00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:40.169
Now, I'm sure you
know what this is.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:43.429
It's the normal modes.

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:47.230
You go through a
frequency response once,

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:48.230
you go through it twice.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.820
You square it, right?

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:53.990
Cascaded the same function with
itself, you get the square.

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.460
And so it goes each time.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:02.300
And if you have a filter that is
a reasonably narrow band, when

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:05.050
you square that thing,
it being comes something

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:08.750
that goes way up here,
becomes a much higher Q.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:10.160
You can figure out--

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.350
I didn't happen to do
it this turn, but it's

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.279
easy to figure out the Q of
these modes of the normal--

00:09:16.279 --> 00:09:17.570
these normal modes of the room.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:21.290
Because you figured out
reverberation time already,

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.230
and that means, as you
saw in the derivation,

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.330
that you turn on a
sine wave in a room

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.580
and it decays exponentially.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.040
Well, that's exactly
what you would

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:38.060
have if you had a two
pole network, which

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.700
has a response that
looks like that anyway.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.450
And twice this distance
is the bandwidth.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:50.630
So twice the decay
rate that it decays at.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.150
The alpha there will
give you the bandwidth

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:55.490
of the normal modes, and
they're pretty high Q.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:59.030
And you may not have noticed
or paid attention to it

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.540
the first time, but it took--

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.210
when we got to the sixth time,
it took time for the sound

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:06.290
to build up.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:10.610
It didn't just start right on
like the original music did.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:11.510
It built up.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:16.100
And then, when it finished,
it died out slowly.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.310
Because the Q of these
filters getting larger

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:24.980
each time caused this fellow
bandwidth to get much smaller.

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:29.780
And therefore, it died out
much, much, much more slowly.

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:32.540
If you try that same experiment
with other equipment,

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:33.530
like an amplifier.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.000
You go through 40 times.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:41.210
And if you adjust it so that
the frequency response is OK--

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.270
I mean, if it's a few
dB down or something,

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.380
then every time
that gets amplified.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:48.360
And then you will hear it.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.270
But if you adjust the
frequency response,

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.010
you can go through
an amplifier 40 times

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.290
and not know it, except
maybe for noise level.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:59.360
And so it's a way of putting
a magnifying glass on what's

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:00.590
causing the problem.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:01.686
One could argue here--

00:11:01.686 --> 00:11:03.560
and you'll just have to
take our word for it,

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:07.120
that we worried about that
and paid attention to it--

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.070
that you're going
through two things.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:11.480
Yes, all the amplifiers and all.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.160
We actually did go
through all of them

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.934
quite a number of times.

00:11:14.934 --> 00:11:16.850
But you're also going
through the loudspeaker.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:20.450
But take my word
for it, that was--

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:21.830
what you heard was not that.

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.710
It was, in fact, the
normal modes of the room.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:34.490
Before changing completely
from the academic side now,

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.970
I just would like to say
a few words with respect

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:42.800
to what I regard as excellent
performance of the teaching

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.000
assistants.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.570
Yesterday, just to
give you an idea,

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.820
there was a meeting
to discuss grades.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:56.270
Now, they actually prepared
all the numbers every

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:59.270
which way you could
possibly look at them--

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.940
columns and columns and columns.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.690
And they-- a meeting
like this could

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:09.350
have lasted 15 minutes, after
all the preparation that

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:10.640
was done.

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:14.360
It lasted five hours.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:17.480
Because there was enormous
discussion in each case,

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:19.315
what do we know
about the individual?

00:12:19.315 --> 00:12:23.120
Do the quizzes really
represent what that person

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.760
knows about the subject?

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.130
Did that person have--
that particular person

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:35.990
have a really bad time in his
term, in which the average came

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.790
down because of that?

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:41.780
And all of that only
happens when somebody cares.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:46.160
And these teaching assistants
have, just like you,

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:48.530
all the exams coming up.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:53.690
They have a lot to do, and they
could have recommended that it

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:57.380
be a 15 minute meeting,
but they didn't.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:04.440
So I have particular thanks
to all of them to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:05.210
Get up.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:06.202
Come on up.

00:13:06.202 --> 00:13:10.130
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:12.106
[INAUDIBLE]

00:13:12.106 --> 00:13:15.058
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:17.026 --> 00:13:18.502
[? Monish ?] [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:18.502 --> 00:13:21.450
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:27.277
To the head teaching assistant,
Carlos [? Cabrera. ?]

00:13:27.277 --> 00:13:27.860
Where are you?

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:31.115
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:31.115 --> 00:13:37.920
And also, by the way, in terms
of hours that they spent,

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.750
they spent many more hours
than they were either

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:47.040
compensated for or
assigned to on this task.

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:50.500
I hope that they have.

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:51.882
I know they enjoyed it.

00:13:51.882 --> 00:13:53.340
I hope they have
learned something,

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.140
and I'm going to
ask each one of them

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.690
if they would like to say
a few words in the moment.

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:00.120
But there's another person
I would like to acknowledge,

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:03.120
and that is Neil
[INAUDIBLE],, who

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.790
was a teaching assistant in
this subject a few times,

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.880
did a magnificent job.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.605
And he-- Neil?

00:14:11.605 --> 00:14:15.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.735
Neil helped out this term
with the Bose people.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:25.010
And the WPI.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.820
He taught the same
course here at Bose.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:33.260
And, as I say, you did a
fantastic job last year

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:38.020
at MIT and the year
before that and here now.

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:38.520
Yes?

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:39.320
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.278
PROFESSOR: [INAUDIBLE]!

00:14:40.278 --> 00:14:41.580
Oh, [INAUDIBLE] isn't here.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:44.250
That's-- yeah, yeah.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.330
By all means.

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:47.310
Is he here?

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:48.131
Oh!

00:14:48.131 --> 00:14:51.979
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:51.979 --> 00:14:57.050
[INAUDIBLE] Sorry about that.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.340
I was looking at the faces
here and he had just gone

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:02.680
to pick up his brother at the--

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:03.810
boy, you must have really--

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:09.850
really traveled.

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:11.050
That's not why I missed you.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:11.560
I forgot.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:16.610
Thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.900
I just wondered if
any of you would like

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:22.240
to just say a couple of words.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.296
[INAUDIBLE],, since
you were last?

00:15:25.296 --> 00:15:26.420
You better come here maybe.

00:15:32.206 --> 00:15:33.930
This is totally
unplanned, so I--

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:41.190
TA: I just wanted to say,
it was a great experience.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:42.606
PROFESSOR: this
works, by the way.

00:15:42.606 --> 00:15:44.262
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:44.262 --> 00:15:44.970
TA: Does it work?

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:46.590
OK, good.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:49.440
It was a-- it's an
experience teaching.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:52.830
I taught last year and I learned
a lot, and taught this year

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.090
and I learned even more.

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:56.790
Neil was my TA when I took
it, so I've certainly learned

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:58.750
quite a bit from him as well.

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.920
And it's really
quite a pleasure.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:07.440
And all the time we put into
it is, from my perspective,

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:08.040
worth it.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.200
And I hope you
feel the same way.

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.860
Many of you as students would
be good teaching assistants.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:17.910
And near the end, we'd
like you to sign up

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:21.270
if you have any
interest for next year.

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:26.160
And it's-- lots of students
who I know sort of bypass

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.650
the teaching assistant role,
because they want to do

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:32.610
research, Dr. Bose has told us.

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:35.670
Having been a teacher, and
knowing how much I enjoy it

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.400
and how much I benefit from
it, I suggest it to all of you,

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:41.380
whether you continue
with your studies or not.

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.160
Because there's nothing
that teaches you

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:45.540
material better than teaching.

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:49.350
And I'll tell you, I
was frightfully nervous

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:50.670
the first time I taught.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:54.760
And each time the semester
starts, I am nervous also.

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:58.710
Because I'm sure most of you
are at a much higher level

00:16:58.710 --> 00:16:59.672
than I am.

00:16:59.672 --> 00:17:02.130
So you just have to stand up
there and be confident and say

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:03.538
no, even if you're right.

00:17:03.538 --> 00:17:07.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.210
So thank you.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:10.443
I really did enjoy it.

00:17:10.443 --> 00:17:14.307
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:22.589
TA: [INAUDIBLE] said
is, I agree with that.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:26.670
I mean, there's really
nothing like teaching.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:29.310
You really don't know how
well you know something

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:32.220
until you have to teach
it to somebody else.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.470
And like I said, it's a
scary experience at first.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.080
And like [INAUDIBLE] said,
this is my second time around.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:40.467
And every single time I think
I've gone through this course,

00:17:40.467 --> 00:17:41.550
I've learned just as much.

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:44.550
I've learned actually more,
I think, in teaching in class

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:47.290
than I had as a student
learning in class.

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:48.249
And it's just--

00:17:48.249 --> 00:17:49.290
I agree with [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:51.360
I mean, if you get
a chance, please

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:53.760
do take the time to teach.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:57.630
It's an incredible
experience, because not only

00:17:57.630 --> 00:17:58.630
do you learn from--

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:01.680
I've learned not
only from teaching

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.150
by listening to Dr. Bose and
the other teaching assistants,

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:07.140
but I learn a lot from you guys.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.510
I mean, everybody sees
problems in different ways.

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:14.620
Everybody has their own unique
solution to a given problem.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:18.420
And the more I see of that,
the more amazed-- the more ways

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:19.860
I see a problem.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.340
And it's just incredible.

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:25.170
So I'd just like to thank Dr.
Bose and the teaching staff

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:28.940
and all of you guys for making
such a great experience for me.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:29.639
Thanks.

00:18:29.639 --> 00:18:31.136
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:31.136 --> 00:18:32.633
PROFESSOR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:42.161
TA: Well, actually, I just want
to agree with [INAUDIBLE] also.

00:18:42.161 --> 00:18:44.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:48.540
I'd like to add that I was also
very nervous when I started out

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:49.560
teaching.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.530
I don't think I would have
been able to stand up and talk

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:55.860
to guys unless I had
taught this semester.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.900
It was an excellent
experience just standing up

00:18:57.900 --> 00:18:59.621
and sort of learning
from students

00:18:59.621 --> 00:19:01.620
and learning from the
other teaching assistants.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.330
And, of course, Dr. Bose.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.630
I was actually taking a number
of classes this semester.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:08.880
And it was a very
busy schedule for me,

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.520
but I think this was the
most enjoyable thing that I

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:12.360
did this semester.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.840
And I don't want to repeat
what [INAUDIBLE] said,

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:17.340
but I'd just like to thank all
of you and the teaching staff

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.750
and, of course, Dr. Bose.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:20.170
Thank you very much once again.

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:22.372
And try teaching if you
can, and even if you're

00:19:22.372 --> 00:19:23.330
taking lots of classes.

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:26.107
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:26.606 --> 00:19:27.074
PROFESSOR: How many did
you take in the end?

00:19:27.074 --> 00:19:27.574
TA: Five.

00:19:27.574 --> 00:19:28.407
PROFESSOR: How many?

00:19:28.407 --> 00:19:29.220
TA: Five.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:30.420
PROFESSOR: Whoa!

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:33.980
She was taking five
classes at the same time.

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:34.770
[INAUDIBLE]

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:45.800
TA: I'm going to tell you guys a
couple of really short stories,

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:48.320
sort of anecdotes,
to sort of illustrate

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:49.700
what the other TAs said.

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:55.100
I can't think of a
better way to make

00:19:55.100 --> 00:20:00.087
an impact on someone's
career and the path

00:20:00.087 --> 00:20:00.920
that they're taking.

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:08.390
Last year, when I took the
class, for a number of reasons,

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.210
I was having a
difficult time at MIT.

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:16.680
And that manifested in all
sorts of problems academically--

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.120
a lack of confidence, a
lack of concentration.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.220
I was really doubting myself.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:28.870
And the class, as it
was taught by Dr. Bose,

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:31.240
really grabbed my interest.

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:34.840
But even though I was sitting
in the lectures in awe,

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:41.500
I would go to the problem
sets and I would get stuck.

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:44.620
I really struggled
with the material.

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:47.590
And the person who really
helped me out big time

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:55.840
and pushed me along and
taught me to believe in myself

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.550
was Neil [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:57.550 --> 00:20:58.889
I mean, he wasn't my TA.

00:20:58.889 --> 00:20:59.680
I just went to him.

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:01.340
I said Neil, I need some help.

00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:06.580
And he spent many,
many hours with me.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:10.390
Some of the best advice he gave
me was, put your pencil down,

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:14.260
look at it, and think about
what you want to do first.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:18.850
And I use that on
every problem now.

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:23.920
And if you want to hear
about how becoming a teaching

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:29.050
assistant can make a difference
in your understanding, when

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:34.820
I took the exams last year,
you guys think you stayed long,

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:37.120
I was always one of
the last ones to leave.

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:39.710
I mean, I stayed
really, really late.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:43.590
And I think I annoyed Neil a
couple of times in doing so.

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:48.190
But when this semester
rolled around, [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:50.980
said look, we need you and
[INAUDIBLE] to beta test

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:52.262
the exam.

00:21:52.262 --> 00:21:54.499
What?

00:21:54.499 --> 00:21:56.290
[INAUDIBLE] I've got
like a full load, man.

00:21:56.290 --> 00:21:58.420
I can't spend six hours
like I did last year.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:21:59.870
There's no way!

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:00.935
I mean, I can't do it.

00:22:00.935 --> 00:22:03.310
He said, [INAUDIBLE],, look,
you've got to take the test.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:04.510
We need your feedback.

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:06.220
You and [INAUDIBLE]
are the only people

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:09.880
that can really give us an idea
of where there are ambiguities,

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.160
what needs to be clarified.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.160
So grudgingly, I took
the test from him.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:18.949
And I set aside six hours,
because I said to myself,

00:22:18.949 --> 00:22:20.740
I'm not going to spend
less than six hours.

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:27.040
I really felt that it was
going to take me that long.

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:29.410
But after having presented
the material in class,

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:32.500
after having sat
with the graduate TAs

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:35.860
and really preparing
the material,

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:37.450
I just noticed that
in lecture I began

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:42.370
to see things which I struggled
with so tremendously last year.

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:45.040
Things became
conceptualized in a way

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:47.890
that I hadn't been able to
think of problems before.

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:55.210
And, I mean, by inspection,
the exam fell apart.

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:56.270
It really did.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:59.177
And it did not take me--

00:22:59.177 --> 00:23:00.760
what the hell, it
took me like an hour

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:04.720
or so to get through the exam
that the years prior had taken

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.600
me like a good five or six.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:12.760
So if you really want to make
a difference in someone's life,

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:14.480
I can't think of a better way.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:16.990
And if you want to
improve upon yourself,

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:18.660
I can't think of a better way.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:19.335
Thanks.

00:23:19.335 --> 00:23:22.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:41.090
TA: Well, I have to say that
I agree with all the TAs.

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:43.070
No, I'm not going to
repeat what they said.

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.180
I think the basic idea
has been communicated,

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:49.160
that if you think you
learned something,

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.970
you really didn't learn
as much as we did.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:56.330
I just want to thank all
the teaching assistants.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:24:00.050
They have been of great help.

00:24:00.050 --> 00:24:02.120
I couldn't have done
it without their help

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:03.660
and their support all the time.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:09.880
And I want to thank my students
for teaching me so much.

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:10.736
Thanks.

00:24:10.736 --> 00:24:17.540
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.470
TA: I just want to take a
minute to thank Dr. Bose.

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:34.070
He's the first person
I've ever met who

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:36.770
cares so much about teaching.

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:39.170
And he's the man who
taught me everything

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:41.796
I know about how to
communicate information

00:24:41.796 --> 00:24:42.920
and how to teach to people.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:44.360
So thank you very much.

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:48.296
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:54.840
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:56.250
So much for the academics.

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:58.650
Now we go on to--

00:24:58.650 --> 00:24:59.390
yes?

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:01.420
AUDIENCE: You forgot somebody.

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:03.330
PROFESSOR: Who did I forget?

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:05.060
AUDIENCE: AJ.

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:07.780
AJ was the TA for
the WPI [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.750
PROFESSOR: Oh, I'm sorry.

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:11.490
I did not--

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:15.715
I was not connected to that
and I'm very, very sorry.

00:25:15.715 --> 00:25:16.590
AUDIENCE: No problem.

00:25:19.866 --> 00:25:21.616
PROFESSOR: Would you
like to say anything?

00:25:21.616 --> 00:25:22.287
AUDIENCE: Ditto.

00:25:22.287 --> 00:25:23.162
PROFESSOR: Ditto, OK.

00:25:23.162 --> 00:25:23.662
Good.

00:25:34.415 --> 00:25:41.840
What faces you in the future
is something that most of you

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:43.340
don't expect.

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:49.890
And yet, statistically,
it's an absolute fact.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:54.830
If you look at the
alumni records,

00:25:54.830 --> 00:26:01.860
you will be amazed at the
dispersion of professions

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:10.170
that students enter that have,
in fact, many cases nothing

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:14.920
to do directly with what they
spent their four or five, six,

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:16.280
seven years studying.

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:25.410
Even in this country, those
people who go into engineering,

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:30.840
the average time
spent by a US graduate

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:34.350
in engineering, in
the field, assuming

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:37.950
that he enters the field
that he was trained for,

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:41.505
the average time that he
spends there is seven years.

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:46.686
In Japan, by the way, it's 20.

00:26:46.686 --> 00:26:48.800
A bit different.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:54.630
Here, many things happen,
some good, some bad.

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.580
In many companies, I think
it's somewhat unfortunate,

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:00.090
but the reward for
a good performance

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:02.160
is to make the person a manager.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:05.610
And all too often, you
lose a wonderful engineer

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:08.360
and you get a terrible manager.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:10.785
But there are many
reasons why this happens.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:15.990
But you should be
prepared for this.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:20.700
And even if you don't
even begin in the field

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:25.910
that you were trained for,
hopefully your education

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:28.080
will enable you
to get to the top.

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:32.030
That's what education
should be all about.

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:34.790
Because the facts
that we may teach

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:39.770
you will be totally irrelevant
if you don't enter that field

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:43.010
and will be mostly
irrelevant if you do.

00:27:43.010 --> 00:27:44.460
Because they will change.

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:45.320
They will evolve.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:49.020
You will develop new things.

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:51.440
And so the facts have
little importance.

00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:54.710
The thought process
is everything.

00:27:54.710 --> 00:27:59.690
And as these changes come,
don't feel that you are--

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:03.510
my god, you know, I wasted four
years or five or six years,

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:04.860
whatever it is.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:07.740
That's not the case.

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:09.950
As I think I mentioned
in class, once

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:16.560
the head of surgery at
Mass General Hospital

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:18.760
was an MIT grad.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:21.170
Now you'll all have a chuckle
if I tell you which course.

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:22.570
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:26.330
It had nothing to do with
his skill with a scalpel.

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:30.080
Basically, he'll tell
you what his education

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:35.330
did for him at MIT is
invaluable in the way he thinks.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:39.260
So what I think I am
going to do is take you

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:43.940
through some
highlights to get you

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:48.740
to appreciate some things about
your career as they come along.

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:53.120
And if you'll excuse me,
I will do this in a way

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:55.310
that I think is most believable.

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:58.580
I don't want to stand up
here and preach to you

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.830
and have you wonder--
wow, and maybe it

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:02.340
is your words or whatnot.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:05.660
So I will use some
personal examples here.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:10.504
And I don't think I'm atypical.

00:29:10.504 --> 00:29:12.170
These same things
have happened to a lot

00:29:12.170 --> 00:29:14.900
of people in different forms.

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:17.150
But it's more
believable if it comes

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:18.800
from somebody about himself.

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:20.910
So I'll spend a
little time on that.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:23.510
And then we'll get to
some of the other topics I

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:28.685
want to get to, like how
do you go out job shopping?

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:36.830
And how do you get
creativity really rolling?

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:41.460
Well, I'd like to illustrate
some changes that come along,

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:44.700
that will come along with high
probability in your lives,

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:48.660
that you may not like.

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:54.980
And we'll talk about
what you can do about it.

00:29:54.980 --> 00:29:59.870
When I finished my masters
here, I went over to Philips

00:29:59.870 --> 00:30:01.715
to work for--

00:30:01.715 --> 00:30:03.800
in Holland for the summer.

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:07.040
And I had arranged a doctorate
thesis and conformal mapping

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:08.870
with [? Gillerman. ?]
By the way,

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:12.620
this group is different
than just the MIT class.

00:30:12.620 --> 00:30:15.390
So some of you will have
heard parts of this,

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:18.350
but I'll try to make that short.

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:21.830
And I came back about
a week or two late,

00:30:21.830 --> 00:30:23.840
but I had this
thesis all arranged

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.120
and a research
assistantship, and I

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:28.640
knew that food would
be on the table

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:32.000
because the research
assistantship was there.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:37.730
And I came rolling into
Building 20 and went to my desk

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:39.480
and there was a little
note on the desk.

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:43.340
It said, please see the director
of the laboratory, research

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:45.050
laboratory of electronics.

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:47.500
So I picked it up and I went in.

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:51.320
It was Dr. Jerome
Wiesner, who was later

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:53.900
President Kennedy's
science advisor

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:57.740
and then President of MIT.

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.790
But he was head of the
research laboratory, RLE,

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:02.780
that exists today.

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:05.150
And so he and the assistant
director were there.

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:10.010
And I sat down and wondered why
I was asked to come in there,

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:12.230
since they had nothing
to do with my thesis,

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:15.260
except I was working
in that laboratory.

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:17.990
But the thesis was all
theoretical, in any case.

00:31:17.990 --> 00:31:22.160
And so they told me the
story about Norbert Wiener.

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:26.540
And I also wondered why they
were telling me that story.

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:28.370
And then it came
down to the fact

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:30.830
that they thought Norbert
Wiener had some things that

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:34.414
were really applicable in
electrical engineering,

00:31:34.414 --> 00:31:36.830
and they'd been thinking--
because he had a number of them

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:38.450
over the decades,
but they thought

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:42.530
he had some material more
recently that they really

00:31:42.530 --> 00:31:48.020
wanted to get, to transfer
into electrical engineering.

00:31:48.020 --> 00:31:51.080
And then they said
that, look, we

00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:54.600
have tried three
doctorates on this program.

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:55.640
And they did all right.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:59.510
But we think that there's
something there still.

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:01.110
And then came the bombshell.

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:03.395
They said, we would like
you to work on this.

00:32:03.395 --> 00:32:05.770
And I was shocked.

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:07.760
And I thought maybe they
didn't know, you know,

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:09.560
that I have a research
assistantship.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:13.910
I have a professor that
I thought the highest of,

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:14.830
and I'm very happy.

00:32:14.830 --> 00:32:17.030
And so I explained
that immediately.

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:20.010
And they said, yes, we know.

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:23.060
But we would like
you to work on this.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:27.140
And I was really
annoyed at this point,

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:28.950
because it was a
totally different field.

00:32:28.950 --> 00:32:30.330
I had taken a minor in math.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:31.850
But, you know, to
work for Norbert

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:34.302
Wiener with a minor
in math is like,

00:32:34.302 --> 00:32:36.760
you know, studying calculus
when you don't know arithmetic.

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:42.390
And so I listened and
I finally said, look,

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:44.270
are you drafting me?

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:47.120
And they said yes.

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:50.630
Well, it's the only time I
know of anybody being drafted

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:51.950
to do a doctorate thesis.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:55.790
And then they could see the
shock on my face at this point.

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:57.920
And they said, well, let's--

00:32:57.920 --> 00:33:00.980
look, if you don't like it
after a year, you come back,

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:04.590
and we'll let you go back to
the thesis you wanted to do.

00:33:04.590 --> 00:33:07.070
And so I was, what?

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:12.110
23 at the time, and I
was really, really upset.

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:15.990
So I said, look, if you're
not going to give me a choice,

00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:18.710
I'm not going to come back
into this office in one year

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:20.060
and start over.

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:22.650
If this is my only
choice, here I go.

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:24.182
So I went out.

00:33:24.182 --> 00:33:25.640
And they told me
that I had to have

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:28.520
a nominal supervisor in
electrical engineering,

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:29.960
because I was in EE.

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:34.790
And so that this would
be Professor YW Lee.

00:33:34.790 --> 00:33:39.100
So I went to see him
and I said, look,

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:40.760
I will see you in a year or two.

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:43.100
I've got to go in the math
department and study math,

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:45.150
since I've been
assigned this project.

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:46.790
He said, no, no, no, no, no.

00:33:46.790 --> 00:33:51.830
So he gave me a
whole pile of-- it

00:33:51.830 --> 00:34:00.240
must have been about 50 pages or
so of typed, mainly equations.

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:02.040
And said, you just read this.

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:02.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:07.890
So I read it.

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:13.409
I read it twice a day
for about six months,

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:17.230
and it didn't make
any sense at all.

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:21.420
So I started to go over to the
math department in the library

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:24.989
and read anything that
I thought was related.

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:27.780
I wasn't even sure what was
related to this mathematics.

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:31.530
So I started reading
everything I could.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:34.271
And I kept coming back to Lee
and telling him, oh, boy, this

00:34:34.271 --> 00:34:34.770
is--

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:38.100
I don't even know
anything about this.

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:39.840
And this is ridiculous.

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:41.800
And he didn't know
anything either.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:45.560
And so he just kept saying,
don't worry, it'll come.

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:48.949
And I did worry, and
it wasn't coming.

00:34:48.949 --> 00:34:51.785
And so, this went on
until about February.

00:34:51.785 --> 00:34:54.690
It started in September and
went on until about February.

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:58.990
And then Lee came into my
office one day and said,

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:00.660
oh, by the way, he
said, there's going

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:04.920
to be an international
conference of mathematicians

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:07.500
here at MIT this summer in June.

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:12.531
And you are to speak
on Wiener theory.

00:35:12.531 --> 00:35:13.854
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:13.854 --> 00:35:15.180
Aie, aie, aie.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:18.960
At this point, I really,
literally, came to the point

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:22.170
where you have these
dreams where you have--

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:24.960
somehow you've wound up
in class in grade school

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:27.220
and you forgot to get dressed.

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:29.990
And how do you get out?

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:34.950
And, you know, it was
just a horror experience.

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:37.320
And I never went to see
Wiener during all this period.

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:39.780
Because what could I say,
that I don't understand,

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:43.440
were about the only words I
could utter to Norbert Wiener.

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:45.788
That's all I knew.

00:35:45.788 --> 00:35:50.370
And so I begged to get out of
this thing, this presentation,

00:35:50.370 --> 00:35:53.310
because these were-- the top
mathematicians from the world

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:56.050
were gathered in
this, as I was told.

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:58.860
And I begged him
to get out of this.

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:02.220
And no, no, don't worry,
don't worry, it'll come.

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:04.600
So I kept reading this thing
twice a day, twice a day.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:07.029
I read it day and
night and tried

00:36:07.029 --> 00:36:09.570
to get my mind off of the fact
that I couldn't understand it.

00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:12.090
Because if you focus
on something like that,

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:13.590
you're dead.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:17.189
If you think you cannot do
something, you will not do it.

00:36:17.189 --> 00:36:18.480
So you've got to get your mind.

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:20.979
You have-- And you're going to
bump into problems like that.

00:36:20.979 --> 00:36:24.750
Get that kind of thinking
out of your head.

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:30.150
Because that is an absolute
block to any kind of progress.

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:32.580
So I kept going, kept going.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:36.342
And I gave up on visiting Lee
after about four or five times.

00:36:36.342 --> 00:36:38.550
I said, look, he's just
going to tell me, keep at it,

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:39.816
it'll come.

00:36:39.816 --> 00:36:46.560
So finally, just two weeks
before, one afternoon I

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:47.340
read it--

00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:51.180
and oh, by the way.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:52.260
I found some mistakes.

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:55.110
As I was beginning to get
a little understanding,

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:58.029
I could understand enough in
these huge integral equations

00:36:58.029 --> 00:36:59.195
that there were some errors.

00:36:59.195 --> 00:37:00.862
At least I thought there were.

00:37:00.862 --> 00:37:02.320
So I went to him,
I said, you know,

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:04.260
this seems like there's
some errors here.

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:05.940
He said, oh I didn't--

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.440
I forgot to tell you.

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:11.250
He said, this was typed
from the back of envelopes

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:13.410
that Norbert Wiener
had scratched on

00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:16.120
in Paris by a secretary
that didn't type English,

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:18.300
let alone math.

00:37:18.300 --> 00:37:20.668
And so he said, you
can expect some errors.

00:37:20.668 --> 00:37:21.168
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:21.168 --> 00:37:23.442
I can tell you.

00:37:23.442 --> 00:37:24.900
At least I thought,
well, you know,

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:28.240
maybe I'm getting to the level
where I can see the errors.

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:33.300
Well, just two weeks before
the conference was to start,

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:34.440
I read it one afternoon.

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:38.040
And bang, everything
became crystal clear.

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:40.830
Just absolutely clear.

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:45.060
And I really sat back that
night and I said, my god,

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:48.640
is Lee a professor or a prophet?

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:54.990
I mean, he had this seemingly
incredible confidence.

00:37:54.990 --> 00:37:58.077
And the conference began,
and I got up there.

00:37:58.077 --> 00:37:59.910
It was the first time
I had talked publicly.

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:03.420
And, oh my god, I mean,
my throat got dry.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:06.360
I was so nervous in the
first couple of minutes I--

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:07.830
and this audience out there.

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:10.460
And Wiener was in the audience.

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:13.560
And I-- boy, I didn't think
I was going to survive.

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:15.930
But after about two or
three minutes of that,

00:38:15.930 --> 00:38:18.060
everything came back
and you started going.

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:22.230
It was fine until somebody
raised their hand.

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:24.570
And what happens when you're
in the front of a group

00:38:24.570 --> 00:38:26.310
and somebody raises their
hand, the first thing you see

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:26.740
is the hand.

00:38:26.740 --> 00:38:27.906
And then you follow it down.

00:38:27.906 --> 00:38:31.080
And guess who it
was connected to?

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:32.940
Norbert Wiener.

00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:35.190
And I thought, at that
point, it was all over.

00:38:35.190 --> 00:38:39.510
I mean, if there had been a
panic button in the podium,

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:41.880
I would have gone
straight through it.

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:43.260
And so he asked the question.

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:47.430
And I was so nervous I couldn't
even listen to the question.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:48.540
I thought I had it.

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:50.010
I answered it incorrectly.

00:38:50.010 --> 00:38:55.792
And very calmly, very politely,
he said exactly the same thing.

00:38:55.792 --> 00:38:58.000
He didn't say, you didn't
answer it or anything else.

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:00.870
He just went through
the same question again.

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:03.960
And somehow, that got to
me, and I calmed down,

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:08.220
and I was able to answer
it, with great fortune.

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:09.750
And then the whole thing turned.

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:14.160
I mean, I went on through
the presentation, just

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:17.380
no problems at all, like
I'd been teaching for years.

00:39:17.380 --> 00:39:20.670
And then at the end of
the whole conference,

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:25.090
they had all the
mathematicians fill out

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:31.150
a form relative to the
value of the whole two week

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:32.890
conference, et cetera.

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:35.050
And one day, Lee
walked into my office

00:39:35.050 --> 00:39:39.250
and he said, maybe you'd like to
know that your presentation was

00:39:39.250 --> 00:39:41.720
voted to be the best.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:43.490
And I thought, my god.

00:39:43.490 --> 00:39:45.500
You know, if they only
knew what was inside

00:39:45.500 --> 00:39:47.540
of me when I was making it.

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:50.720
And also, you know, I'm
not a mathematician.

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:56.210
And it turns out that you can
do things in other fields.

00:39:56.210 --> 00:39:57.020
You really can.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:39:59.360
That made a huge
difference in my life.

00:39:59.360 --> 00:40:04.820
Because when I realized for
the first time that, gee,

00:40:04.820 --> 00:40:08.360
you could come to a
level in another field

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:12.020
that you didn't know
anything about a year ago--

00:40:12.020 --> 00:40:16.110
with intense effort, to be
sure, and a lot of time spent--

00:40:16.110 --> 00:40:18.440
but it gives you--
whenever that happens,

00:40:18.440 --> 00:40:22.070
it gives you a confidence that
you are now on another plateau,

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:25.301
and you're ready to go up again.

00:40:25.301 --> 00:40:27.800
And it takes away a lot of the
fear that you would have had.

00:40:27.800 --> 00:40:29.450
I went through more
fear in that year

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:31.260
than you could ever imagine.

00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:34.220
And that started a
10-year relationship

00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:39.230
between Norbert Wiener and I in
which we met almost every day,

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:42.530
and it was something that
everything I've ever earned

00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:46.760
could never even
begin to pay for.

00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:53.630
Well, when I graduated, or when
I was doing the final thesis

00:40:53.630 --> 00:40:58.340
exam, Wiesner, Jerry Wiesner,
was one of the examiners.

00:40:58.340 --> 00:41:00.140
And after the exam--

00:41:00.140 --> 00:41:04.730
it was over in the math
department, I think it was.

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:09.080
And we had a long way to
walk back to Building 26.

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.060
And he asked me,
what am I doing?

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:14.120
Well, it turned out that
in February of the year--

00:41:14.120 --> 00:41:15.570
I was going to graduate in June.

00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:21.880
And February, Norbert
Wiener had said to me--

00:41:21.880 --> 00:41:24.170
at that time, by the
way, my frame of mind

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.050
was, I couldn't wait to
get out of the industry

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:28.550
and get a research job.

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:33.890
I had studiously avoided
all forms of teaching.

00:41:33.890 --> 00:41:37.200
I knew that was the one thing
that I never wanted to do.

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:38.680
And so I was certain of that.

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:44.090
And all teaching assistantships
weren't of any interest.

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:48.320
I always wanted to research,
and I lived on that.

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:51.110
And then-- and I couldn't wait
to get out there to do it.

00:41:51.110 --> 00:41:53.830
And in February or
January of that year

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:56.510
that I was going to graduate,
Norbert Wiener one day

00:41:56.510 --> 00:42:00.320
said, you know, Bose, your
heritage is from India.

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:03.170
You should spend a year there.

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:05.240
Apply for a Fulbright.

00:42:05.240 --> 00:42:07.920
Well, if anybody else in the
world had said that to me,

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:09.720
I would have fought
them tooth and nail.

00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:12.650
But I had come to respect
him so highly by that time

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:14.450
I found myself
saying, all right.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:15.410
Without even thinking.

00:42:15.410 --> 00:42:17.490
I mean, if my parents had
told me that or anybody else

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:18.531
had told me that, oh god.

00:42:18.531 --> 00:42:19.160
You know?

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.500
Because I was afraid of
spending any time away

00:42:21.500 --> 00:42:24.500
from this that your
level would go down,

00:42:24.500 --> 00:42:27.300
without exercising
the math and whatnot.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:30.080
And so I found myself just
like that saying, all right.

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:30.987
And I did it.

00:42:30.987 --> 00:42:33.320
But then, a couple months
later, I got a little worried.

00:42:33.320 --> 00:42:35.470
I said, you know, suppose I--

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:39.470
I'm applying for this thing
now, and suppose I don't get it.

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:41.460
Oh, boy, the end of
the line comes in June.

00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:46.130
I don't have a job
and I have no income.

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:47.010
So I went to Lee--

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:50.650
Professor Lee-- and I told
him, there's a problem.

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:53.110
I think I'd better start
going out interviewing just

00:42:53.110 --> 00:42:56.050
to back this thing up.

00:42:56.050 --> 00:42:58.570
With the same confidence, he
says, don't worry, it'll come.

00:42:58.570 --> 00:43:01.830
He said, don't do that.

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:06.790
He said, Norbert Wiener is
writing the recommendation.

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:12.120
So I took him for his
word, and then it happened.

00:43:12.120 --> 00:43:14.970
It all came.

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:20.330
But at the graduation in
June, coming out of the exam,

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:22.780
Jerry Wiesner said to me--

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:24.030
he asked me what I was doing.

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:25.321
And I told him the whole story.

00:43:25.321 --> 00:43:29.310
I was going to India
for a year, and that I

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:31.380
was going to use the
summer to make certain

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:34.650
that I found the job I want in
industry so that the minute I

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:37.050
come back I'm all set.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.230
And he said, well,
what about teaching?

00:43:39.230 --> 00:43:41.720
And I explained to him
why I didn't want anything

00:43:41.720 --> 00:43:43.110
to do with that.

00:43:43.110 --> 00:43:46.470
And so we walked a few more
paces down the corridor,

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:48.330
and he said, in
those days, you used

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:52.430
to have to be an instructor--
a teaching assistant,

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.480
and then an instructor,
and then faculty.

00:43:54.480 --> 00:43:56.160
And so he said,
well, look, suppose

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:58.781
we would bypass these things
and put you on the faculty.

00:43:58.781 --> 00:44:00.780
And I said, well, you
know, thank you very much,

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:04.960
but I don't want anything
to do with teaching.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:06.510
And I know what I want.

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.500
I want research.

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:10.140
And that ended the conversation.

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:12.570
And then we walked
a little further.

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:15.160
And I thought, you know, they
were so kind to offer all this.

00:44:15.160 --> 00:44:17.160
Maybe I could ask for
something I really wanted.

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:18.921
I said, could I
stay in the summer?

00:44:18.921 --> 00:44:21.420
I have a little money saved
from the research assistantship.

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:22.800
I can live.

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:25.140
And could I stay with no salary.

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:28.290
I just bought a loudspeaker, and
the thing measured very well,

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:30.030
and it sounded horrible.

00:44:30.030 --> 00:44:32.310
And I want to find out why.

00:44:32.310 --> 00:44:36.390
And if I could stay here
and use your equipment,

00:44:36.390 --> 00:44:38.670
I don't want a
salary, but I do--

00:44:38.670 --> 00:44:41.130
would like to use the anechoic
chamber and equipment.

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:42.510
And he said, absolutely.

00:44:42.510 --> 00:44:43.810
No problem.

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:46.470
So that was the end of the
conversation, and I did that.

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:49.950
And that started to
change my career sort of.

00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:55.560
But another change I never
expected to be in this area.

00:44:55.560 --> 00:44:59.250
But then I-- a
week or so before--

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:03.910
the two weeks before the plane
was to leave in September,

00:45:03.910 --> 00:45:06.450
I was in the pool
at MIT, and I was

00:45:06.450 --> 00:45:08.600
coming to the end of the lane.

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:12.540
And somebody-- I
don't remember who.

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:15.810
I think it may have
been Thomas [INAUDIBLE],,

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:18.670
who is of digital fame today.

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:22.140
But he reaches arm over
the edge of the pool

00:45:22.140 --> 00:45:25.410
and said, congratulations.

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:26.922
And we went through a big thing.

00:45:26.922 --> 00:45:28.130
It seemed to be interminable.

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:30.360
It must have lasted
a minute or so.

00:45:30.360 --> 00:45:30.910
For what?

00:45:30.910 --> 00:45:32.280
No you know what!

00:45:32.280 --> 00:45:35.250
No, I don't.

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:37.630
Finally, he was convinced
I didn't know what.

00:45:37.630 --> 00:45:41.246
And so he said, well, he
said, the full catalog is out.

00:45:41.246 --> 00:45:42.870
And you're on the
faculty, with a leave

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:45.555
of absence for the first year.

00:45:45.555 --> 00:45:47.640
Then I thought,
you know, come on.

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:51.690
This is-- now I thought
he was pulling my leg.

00:45:51.690 --> 00:45:55.080
And another minute
or so, he sort of

00:45:55.080 --> 00:45:56.590
convinced me it was true.

00:45:56.590 --> 00:45:59.370
So when I was in the
shower room and changing,

00:45:59.370 --> 00:46:01.500
I decided what to do
about this strange thing.

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:04.710
And I decided, before
I left the pool, that I

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:08.180
would do it for two years.

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:11.720
And I was dreadfully
afraid of absence.

00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:13.130
Then I would be three years.

00:46:13.130 --> 00:46:15.070
I would go away one
year and two years.

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:18.530
I thought, oh my god, I'll be
done, as far as research goes.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:20.600
Because in the faculty,
there's so many duties

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:24.780
that I don't have the time
that I wanted to have.

00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:26.960
And so I went to Lee.

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:29.270
I never even-- I didn't
even know who to go to.

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:31.639
And I went to Lee
and I said, you know,

00:46:31.639 --> 00:46:33.680
I've been told that this
thing is in the catalog.

00:46:33.680 --> 00:46:37.210
And you know, I think
I'll do it for two years.

00:46:37.210 --> 00:46:38.870
Well, as far as I
know, to this day

00:46:38.870 --> 00:46:46.270
there's never been any agreement
between MIT and myself.

00:46:46.270 --> 00:46:46.770
I did it.

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:48.144
I didn't even know
how much I was

00:46:48.144 --> 00:46:49.470
going to get until I came back.

00:46:49.470 --> 00:46:52.550
And at the end of
the first month

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:55.860
I think there was a paycheck.

00:46:55.860 --> 00:47:00.000
So I decided to do it
only for two years.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.180
Then what happened
was something that I

00:47:02.180 --> 00:47:06.350
didn't realize until a couple
of years ago, what changed that.

00:47:06.350 --> 00:47:07.700
And it was in the second term.

00:47:07.700 --> 00:47:10.130
I was teaching a course
in statistical theory

00:47:10.130 --> 00:47:12.390
of communication, grad course.

00:47:12.390 --> 00:47:14.600
And it was a big grad course.

00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:16.430
And there was another
professor teaching it

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:18.710
who had been a teacher of mine.

00:47:18.710 --> 00:47:23.870
And being a junior, I had to
do all the sorting of the cards

00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:25.200
that came in and whatnot.

00:47:25.200 --> 00:47:28.220
And they were-- out
of about 50 students,

00:47:28.220 --> 00:47:32.390
there were three or
four of them who said,

00:47:32.390 --> 00:47:37.230
on their card, no offense, but I
would like Professor So and So.

00:47:37.230 --> 00:47:40.130
He was a very good teacher.

00:47:40.130 --> 00:47:43.810
And, well, you know, when
you read that, one feeling,

00:47:43.810 --> 00:47:45.510
sort of, it hurts you.

00:47:45.510 --> 00:47:48.796
But the next minute, you
realize, look, you know,

00:47:48.796 --> 00:47:49.670
he's very well known.

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:53.420
He is a good teacher, and
the student was very polite

00:47:53.420 --> 00:47:56.840
about saying, no offense,
but I would like that person.

00:47:56.840 --> 00:47:58.640
Well, what went
through my mind then--

00:47:58.640 --> 00:48:00.440
and I never realized
that this is

00:48:00.440 --> 00:48:03.140
what changed my decision until
just a couple of years ago.

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:05.435
For some reason, I had
to give a talk between--

00:48:05.435 --> 00:48:07.000
it was even more recent.

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:11.900
Had to give a talk at the
centenary of his birth.

00:48:11.900 --> 00:48:14.060
What happened was
I said to myself--

00:48:14.060 --> 00:48:17.900
and I remember that now,
that, you know, one day, I'm

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:20.420
going to try to become such
a good teacher that students

00:48:20.420 --> 00:48:22.340
might say, no offense
to somebody else,

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:23.890
they would like to have me.

00:48:23.890 --> 00:48:26.690
And saying that
was a commitment.

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:28.760
Because you weren't
going to achieve that

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:31.150
in one more year by any means.

00:48:31.150 --> 00:48:32.150
So that's what happened.

00:48:32.150 --> 00:48:36.920
And so I went into the--
marched into the classroom.

00:48:36.920 --> 00:48:39.240
And I was a professor
by definition.

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:41.510
I didn't know anything
about it and had

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:46.110
to try to learn what
it meant to teach.

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:48.600
But I found it interesting.

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:54.780
And found it interesting
to the extent that--

00:48:54.780 --> 00:48:58.080
this was 1957 when I got back--

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:00.960
to the extent that
when patents had

00:49:00.960 --> 00:49:05.130
issued in electronics
and acoustics,

00:49:05.130 --> 00:49:09.424
and it came time that MIT
had to decide what they

00:49:09.424 --> 00:49:10.590
were going to do with these.

00:49:10.590 --> 00:49:14.060
If they take them at that
time-- and may be the same now--

00:49:14.060 --> 00:49:17.010
you get 12% of the
royalties that they get.

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:19.680
And if they decide not
to take the patents,

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:21.490
they give them to the faculty.

00:49:21.490 --> 00:49:23.130
Well, they gave them to me.

00:49:23.130 --> 00:49:26.220
And I-- these were
patents in electronics,

00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:32.910
and switching amplifiers and
other patents and acoustics.

00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:34.410
So I thought, ah.

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:36.540
Now, by this time,
I really realized

00:49:36.540 --> 00:49:38.110
I wanted to stay at MIT.

00:49:38.110 --> 00:49:39.860
So let's license these patents.

00:49:39.860 --> 00:49:43.792
So my former roommate at
MIT had gone on to Harvard

00:49:43.792 --> 00:49:45.000
and become a patent attorney.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:48.090
And so he started to
try to license these

00:49:48.090 --> 00:49:50.076
with absolutely no luck at all.

00:49:50.076 --> 00:49:51.450
Because the acoustic
patents were

00:49:51.450 --> 00:49:56.160
useful for the home and
the electronics patents

00:49:56.160 --> 00:49:58.170
at that time were only
useful for government,

00:49:58.170 --> 00:50:03.780
because they were high
efficiency power processing

00:50:03.780 --> 00:50:07.020
and amplification
kinds of devices

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:11.290
that were very expensive--
lightweight, very, very small.

00:50:11.290 --> 00:50:12.840
I mean, you could--

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:15.880
we demonstrated the 21 amplifier
that was only that big.

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:18.820
You've seen better
than that now.

00:50:18.820 --> 00:50:22.600
And so he was getting
absolutely nowhere.

00:50:22.600 --> 00:50:24.380
And one day, Lee--

00:50:24.380 --> 00:50:26.820
Professor Lee, the head of
electrical engineering--

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:29.790
he was a very, very
cultured Chinese gentleman.

00:50:29.790 --> 00:50:32.700
And he brought me into his
office and he sat me down.

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:34.740
And he said, I would
like-- out of the blue,

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:38.760
he said, I would like to
tell you about my time

00:50:38.760 --> 00:50:42.854
during the war, World
War II, in China.

00:50:42.854 --> 00:50:44.520
I have no idea why
he wanted to do this.

00:50:44.520 --> 00:50:47.040
We normally talked
about technical things.

00:50:47.040 --> 00:50:47.730
So he started.

00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:51.060
And he said, I could see
that there was a war coming

00:50:51.060 --> 00:50:52.530
within '39, or something.

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:54.070
The Japanese were coming in.

00:50:54.070 --> 00:50:56.530
And he said, I felt--
he was a professor.

00:50:56.530 --> 00:50:58.800
He had graduated under
Norbert Wiener, gone back

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:02.550
to teach in what was Peking.

00:51:02.550 --> 00:51:05.940
And he decided he better
get out of the country.

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:09.420
So he came-- and he was a
very conservative person.

00:51:09.420 --> 00:51:12.110
He came three days to
Shanghai before the ship came.

00:51:14.667 --> 00:51:15.750
And he told me about this.

00:51:15.750 --> 00:51:18.660
And the next day, after
he arrived in his hotel,

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:20.950
the Japanese arrived.

00:51:20.950 --> 00:51:22.950
And so he never got
out of that hotel

00:51:22.950 --> 00:51:25.020
for the duration of the war.

00:51:25.020 --> 00:51:29.490
Out of it meaning to
continue his journey.

00:51:29.490 --> 00:51:31.470
And he had to survive,
because the money

00:51:31.470 --> 00:51:35.130
that he had wasn't adequate
to keep him in the hotel.

00:51:35.130 --> 00:51:38.590
So he became-- from a professor
of electrical engineering,

00:51:38.590 --> 00:51:42.300
he became a curio dealer.

00:51:42.300 --> 00:51:45.660
And he decided to tell me
all about the curio business.

00:51:45.660 --> 00:51:49.980
And I'm still wondering, why
is he telling me all this?

00:51:49.980 --> 00:51:52.380
But I knew him from the past.

00:51:52.380 --> 00:51:56.060
He always had a vision that
was a little bit further.

00:51:56.060 --> 00:51:59.010
And so he described that
they go up to the hills,

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:02.730
and they go to the farmers.

00:52:02.730 --> 00:52:04.800
And the farmers
dig up many things

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:09.090
just in plowing that are
curios that are of value.

00:52:09.090 --> 00:52:11.160
And they take them to
town and they market them.

00:52:11.160 --> 00:52:13.360
That's what he did.

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:17.360
And it's a tough business.

00:52:17.360 --> 00:52:19.220
He was reduced to a
string bean, literally.

00:52:19.220 --> 00:52:20.830
I saw pictures of him.

00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:24.297
It was like a walking
skeleton at the time.

00:52:24.297 --> 00:52:26.130
But in any case, he
carried on his business.

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:27.546
And then he told
me, you know what

00:52:27.546 --> 00:52:29.090
the dream of a curio dealer is?

00:52:29.090 --> 00:52:30.990
He said, it has two parts.

00:52:30.990 --> 00:52:33.440
First part is that one day
they will go up to the hills

00:52:33.440 --> 00:52:39.950
and an article of great value
will come into their hands.

00:52:39.950 --> 00:52:42.950
Somebody digs up something
that really has value.

00:52:42.950 --> 00:52:46.850
Second part of the dream is that
they will recognize the value

00:52:46.850 --> 00:52:52.060
and not just pass it
through as another curio.

00:52:52.060 --> 00:52:56.150
And then he said, that's
all I wanted to tell you.

00:52:56.150 --> 00:52:59.702
He didn't turn me out, but that
was the end of the discussion.

00:53:03.010 --> 00:53:05.110
And I went home and
thought about it,

00:53:05.110 --> 00:53:08.140
and it was pretty clear what
he was really saying to me.

00:53:08.140 --> 00:53:09.280
Without ever mentioning it.

00:53:09.280 --> 00:53:11.560
It was, look, you're trying
to license something.

00:53:11.560 --> 00:53:13.030
You have the curio in your hand.

00:53:13.030 --> 00:53:17.890
It's very valuable, and
you're trying to license it.

00:53:17.890 --> 00:53:19.300
Be careful.

00:53:19.300 --> 00:53:22.920
So I came back in the
next day, and I told him.

00:53:22.920 --> 00:53:26.170
I said, I thought about what
you told me, and I understand.

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:28.720
And then he and I
and then Sherwin

00:53:28.720 --> 00:53:32.060
joined, who was my
student at the time.

00:53:32.060 --> 00:53:36.450
And we had-- and then
my former roommate,

00:53:36.450 --> 00:53:37.450
who had gone to Harvard.

00:53:37.450 --> 00:53:39.610
And we had a number of
sessions about once a month,

00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:41.920
the blind leading the
blind, absolutely,

00:53:41.920 --> 00:53:43.870
about forming a company.

00:53:43.870 --> 00:53:45.310
None of us had to--

00:53:45.310 --> 00:53:49.540
maybe one had read a P&amp;L
once, but I certainly hadn't.

00:53:49.540 --> 00:53:52.060
And what should we do?

00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:54.250
Which of these patents
should we go after?

00:53:54.250 --> 00:53:55.330
And all this and that.

00:53:55.330 --> 00:53:56.290
And it was literally--

00:53:56.290 --> 00:53:59.470
I mean, it would
make a comedy strip

00:53:59.470 --> 00:54:02.140
if it had ever
been tape recorded.

00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:05.630
But that is finally
what led to the company.

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:13.660
Now, what happens in
life is that you're

00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:18.310
dealt many cards which
you don't expect.

00:54:18.310 --> 00:54:22.240
You get changed in your career
from one thing to another.

00:54:22.240 --> 00:54:24.790
There are some people
who, doesn't matter.

00:54:24.790 --> 00:54:28.870
Whichever way they're pointed,
they go after it with gusto

00:54:28.870 --> 00:54:30.370
and they rise to the top.

00:54:30.370 --> 00:54:33.250
And there are some
people who spend

00:54:33.250 --> 00:54:35.770
a good deal of their life
moaning and complaining

00:54:35.770 --> 00:54:39.440
that they were
dealt a poor hand.

00:54:39.440 --> 00:54:42.100
I'm convinced, not only
from my own experiences

00:54:42.100 --> 00:54:45.250
but from watching many,
many people over the years,

00:54:45.250 --> 00:54:46.960
that there is no bad hand.

00:54:46.960 --> 00:54:54.120
That you can optimize almost
anything you set your mind to.

00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:58.620
And you all have a
phenomenal training.

00:54:58.620 --> 00:55:02.940
With that training, your
probability of success

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:04.620
is very high.

00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:10.390
And the jobs that
you get will be--

00:55:10.390 --> 00:55:13.670
not what is given to you,
but what you make of it.

00:55:13.670 --> 00:55:15.530
I'll give you an example--

00:55:15.530 --> 00:55:17.920
I should say two examples.

00:55:17.920 --> 00:55:23.020
For a number of years, I
supervised the Core 6A students

00:55:23.020 --> 00:55:26.600
in two companies, General
Electric and Philco.

00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:27.980
And it was the
Valley Forge Space

00:55:27.980 --> 00:55:30.160
Cetner in General Electric.

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:33.910
And they went through a very bad
period in the '60s where they

00:55:33.910 --> 00:55:34.495
had--

00:55:34.495 --> 00:55:38.140
a Skybolt contract was canceled.

00:55:38.140 --> 00:55:41.440
And they have a contract
with MIT, the 6A companies.

00:55:41.440 --> 00:55:45.400
They have to pay MIT so much
once they sign this thing,

00:55:45.400 --> 00:55:47.980
and they have to take a
certain number of students.

00:55:47.980 --> 00:55:50.020
Well, they were in a very
embarrassing position,

00:55:50.020 --> 00:55:53.650
because they had to take
these students that they

00:55:53.650 --> 00:55:56.260
had no work for.

00:55:56.260 --> 00:55:57.400
So they did.

00:55:57.400 --> 00:56:00.980
And it was my turn to go
down and visit the students.

00:56:00.980 --> 00:56:03.430
And I went down and the
first student came in.

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:04.430
They were good students.

00:56:04.430 --> 00:56:05.674
There were two of them.

00:56:05.674 --> 00:56:07.090
First student came
into the office

00:56:07.090 --> 00:56:10.660
and he said, oh, this
is hell, he said.

00:56:10.660 --> 00:56:12.034
This is ridiculous.

00:56:12.034 --> 00:56:13.200
He said, I want out of this.

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:15.310
He said, you know what?

00:56:15.310 --> 00:56:17.980
He said, they lost the
contract, this Skybolt contract.

00:56:17.980 --> 00:56:20.300
They don't have any work to do.

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:23.470
My boss has been
fired, laid off.

00:56:23.470 --> 00:56:25.570
And he said, you know
what they have me doing?

00:56:25.570 --> 00:56:29.320
He said, they have me sitting
at the end of an assembly line

00:56:29.320 --> 00:56:32.200
and plugging these
transistors into a socket

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:33.790
as they come down.

00:56:33.790 --> 00:56:37.120
And I see that the light is
green, I put them in one bag.

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:40.180
And if it's red, I put
them in the other bag.

00:56:40.180 --> 00:56:41.920
And he said, this
is stupid stuff,

00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:45.400
and he said, I want
out of this program.

00:56:45.400 --> 00:56:48.880
And I got both
barrels, I'll tell you.

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:51.770
And so-- but what could we do?

00:56:51.770 --> 00:56:53.290
There was no work.

00:56:53.290 --> 00:56:56.950
The boss really had been fired.

00:56:56.950 --> 00:56:59.660
So, you know, I said, look,
I'll do the best I can.

00:56:59.660 --> 00:57:05.780
But I have all your inputs now,
and we'll see what we can do.

00:57:05.780 --> 00:57:09.114
And then I had a few
minutes before the next--

00:57:09.114 --> 00:57:10.030
the other boy came in.

00:57:10.030 --> 00:57:13.060
And I looked over his file.

00:57:13.060 --> 00:57:16.720
And, oh, boy, he was
even a better student.

00:57:16.720 --> 00:57:19.100
So I thought, oh
god, here we go.

00:57:19.100 --> 00:57:20.860
I'm really going to
get it this time.

00:57:20.860 --> 00:57:24.530
And so he came in and
I didn't say anything.

00:57:24.530 --> 00:57:28.040
I just, well, how's it going?

00:57:28.040 --> 00:57:32.710
And he said, well, he said, you
know, they lost the contract,

00:57:32.710 --> 00:57:35.740
and my boss has been fired.

00:57:35.740 --> 00:57:40.040
And they're laying off people
left and right around here.

00:57:40.040 --> 00:57:44.440
And he said, you know, but this
is the most worthwhile term

00:57:44.440 --> 00:57:45.850
I've ever had [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:45.850 --> 00:57:48.900
He said, this has been
phenomenal for me.

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:54.580
[INAUDIBLE],, boy,
what is going on?

00:57:54.580 --> 00:57:58.090
And he said, well, you know
what they had me doing?

00:57:58.090 --> 00:57:59.850
No.

00:57:59.850 --> 00:58:01.780
I am sitting at the
end of an assembly line

00:58:01.780 --> 00:58:04.090
and putting these
transistors in there.

00:58:04.090 --> 00:58:06.530
And he said, if the light's
green, I put them in one bag,

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:09.670
and if the light's red, I
put them in another bag.

00:58:09.670 --> 00:58:14.950
So by this time, I wondered
if I was really sitting there.

00:58:14.950 --> 00:58:17.860
And then he said, but
you know, what I did is,

00:58:17.860 --> 00:58:21.460
I went to the management
and I asked him

00:58:21.460 --> 00:58:24.340
if the ones that go in the
red bag, namely the bad ones,

00:58:24.340 --> 00:58:26.870
I could take home.

00:58:26.870 --> 00:58:27.977
And they said, sure.

00:58:27.977 --> 00:58:29.310
They're going to throw them out.

00:58:29.310 --> 00:58:32.180
And he said, I
bought a microscope.

00:58:32.180 --> 00:58:37.080
And I bought some tools to
section these transistors.

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:44.470
And I watched and tried to learn
what was causing the defect.

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:46.540
And he said, in
this term, I have

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:50.740
learned so much that I would
not have learned at MIT,

00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:53.170
that I would not have learned
under other conditions

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:54.580
at the job.

00:58:54.580 --> 00:58:57.580
He said it has been the
best experience in work

00:58:57.580 --> 00:58:59.600
that I have had.

00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:03.720
And I then thought, oh
my god, look at this.

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:06.250
Same exact job.

00:59:06.250 --> 00:59:12.210
1A enjoyed it-- not the actual
plugging of the transistor,

00:59:12.210 --> 00:59:14.310
but the whole experience.

00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:18.150
And he learned an
enormous amount from it.

00:59:18.150 --> 00:59:21.560
And the other one
looked at it as

00:59:21.560 --> 00:59:23.120
if I'd been dealt a bad hand.

00:59:23.120 --> 00:59:25.460
Somebody else gave
me the business,

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:28.370
and what a hell of
a position this is.

00:59:28.370 --> 00:59:32.510
And that was my most
vivid lesson of,

00:59:32.510 --> 00:59:37.450
the job is what you make it.

00:59:37.450 --> 00:59:38.560
And you can take--

00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:40.690
I can't think of too
many things that you

00:59:40.690 --> 00:59:44.656
might be dealt in your life
in terms of jobs that are--

00:59:44.656 --> 00:59:48.220
that are, in some
sense, worse than what

00:59:48.220 --> 00:59:51.340
this person did was given.

00:59:51.340 --> 00:59:52.770
But look at the difference.

00:59:52.770 --> 00:59:56.840
And I somehow wish today
I could follow those two

00:59:56.840 --> 01:00:02.060
and find out what their relative
progress in life has been.

01:00:02.060 --> 01:00:09.310
And I think you can bet which
one has really, really risen.

01:00:09.310 --> 01:00:13.710
So I've always found,
in this company as well,

01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:22.410
that the people who just don't
have the excitement or interest

01:00:22.410 --> 01:00:26.760
and complain about what
they're doing, you can--

01:00:26.760 --> 01:00:28.510
they think they want
to do something else,

01:00:28.510 --> 01:00:29.801
you put them in that direction.

01:00:29.801 --> 01:00:34.000
And sometimes it works,
but usually it doesn't.

01:00:34.000 --> 01:00:36.750
And the really best
people are ones

01:00:36.750 --> 01:00:41.860
who, you could come up to
them today and say, look,

01:00:41.860 --> 01:00:44.880
we really need you who are
going in this direction

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:46.840
to go in that direction.

01:00:46.840 --> 01:00:48.900
And by God, they will
go in that direction,

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:50.850
and they will come to the top.

01:00:50.850 --> 01:00:54.060
I've seen it where technology
is involved, where just business

01:00:54.060 --> 01:00:56.190
is involved.

01:00:56.190 --> 01:01:01.350
They grab the vocabulary of the
field, apply their thinking,

01:01:01.350 --> 01:01:09.990
and they go at enormous rate
and with enormous quality.

01:01:09.990 --> 01:01:12.330
And so it's my feeling
that a good person,

01:01:12.330 --> 01:01:14.730
you can spin him around, in
any direction which he ends,

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:17.820
he will just pursue
with excellence.

01:01:17.820 --> 01:01:21.060
And we all have the
ability to do that.

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:24.420
And that's-- before I talk a
little bit about how you can

01:01:24.420 --> 01:01:26.700
unlock-- how I believe you
can unlock some of that--

01:01:26.700 --> 01:01:32.230
I'd like to just give
you some examples.

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:41.460
Some of you have heard the
talk that I gave in the stand

01:01:41.460 --> 01:01:46.950
and deliver introduction and
will hear some duplication.

01:01:46.950 --> 01:01:48.870
But there were
really, in summary,

01:01:48.870 --> 01:01:51.930
three major
experiences in my life

01:01:51.930 --> 01:01:54.660
that convinced me of something
that if I could convince you

01:01:54.660 --> 01:01:57.980
of in the next 10 minutes,
I would be so happy.

01:01:57.980 --> 01:02:00.990
Because I know it's true.

01:02:00.990 --> 01:02:07.410
Namely that every one of you
has ability in proportions

01:02:07.410 --> 01:02:10.920
that are enormous compared
to what you believe

01:02:10.920 --> 01:02:13.160
and what you've ever used.

01:02:13.160 --> 01:02:17.010
And the first experience that
really drove that home to me

01:02:17.010 --> 01:02:17.820
was Norbert Wiener.

01:02:17.820 --> 01:02:21.090
Because he, in those 10
years of association,

01:02:21.090 --> 01:02:26.920
we did projects together that
covered all sorts of things,

01:02:26.920 --> 01:02:32.860
including the Boston Arm,
which works from your brain.

01:02:32.860 --> 01:02:35.730
And it wound us up
in operating theaters

01:02:35.730 --> 01:02:38.179
in Mass General Hospital.

01:02:38.179 --> 01:02:39.720
A lot of which, by
the way, has never

01:02:39.720 --> 01:02:42.850
been published because
we were scared stiff.

01:02:42.850 --> 01:02:47.280
The people that-- what we
did is the surgeons and--

01:02:47.280 --> 01:02:53.520
I was the head of his group at
Harvard MIT and Mass General.

01:02:53.520 --> 01:02:56.430
And the surgeons
were from Harvard.

01:02:58.290 --> 01:03:02.340
If people needed a operation
that would involve their arm.

01:03:02.340 --> 01:03:08.230
Like if they had a neuroma or if
they needed an older transplant

01:03:08.230 --> 01:03:11.680
through calcification,
we would tell them, look,

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:13.595
we will give you
the operation free

01:03:13.595 --> 01:03:15.720
if you will allow us to
make some experiments which

01:03:15.720 --> 01:03:17.050
won't hurt you.

01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:18.600
We wanted to study
what was actually

01:03:18.600 --> 01:03:20.725
happening in the nerves
and what you could measure.

01:03:20.725 --> 01:03:23.460
And we'd ask these people
who had no arm, for example,

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:26.770
to move their first finger
and their little finger

01:03:26.770 --> 01:03:30.920
and their thumb, and make
believe they had an arm.

01:03:30.920 --> 01:03:34.590
Or if it was an organ
transplant, they actually did.

01:03:34.590 --> 01:03:36.690
And we wanted to study
the nerve transmissions

01:03:36.690 --> 01:03:39.300
so that we could make this
arm as realistic as possible

01:03:39.300 --> 01:03:41.790
working from the brain.

01:03:41.790 --> 01:03:44.010
And we didn't publish
it, because everybody

01:03:44.010 --> 01:03:48.540
was scared stiff that some
ambulance chasing lawyer would

01:03:48.540 --> 01:03:50.757
go to one of these
papers later and suggest

01:03:50.757 --> 01:03:53.340
that he had a headache, and that
the headache was due to this,

01:03:53.340 --> 01:03:54.923
and you could get
millions of dollars,

01:03:54.923 --> 01:03:57.270
as you can see from the
advertisements of many lawyers

01:03:57.270 --> 01:03:58.890
on television.

01:03:58.890 --> 01:04:02.327
In any case, in this
process, before we ever

01:04:02.327 --> 01:04:03.660
got near the operating theaters.

01:04:03.660 --> 01:04:07.020
We had many discussions.

01:04:07.020 --> 01:04:12.780
I think it was every
week in Mass General.

01:04:12.780 --> 01:04:16.630
And after a couple of months
of this, on one break,

01:04:16.630 --> 01:04:18.570
Norbert Wiener was
out of the room.

01:04:18.570 --> 01:04:22.500
And the surgeons, who are
the top of orthopedics

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:26.430
at Mass General, rushed
over to me and said, look.

01:04:26.430 --> 01:04:30.750
They said, you know, we have
heard that Norman Wiener has

01:04:30.750 --> 01:04:32.500
no medical training.

01:04:32.500 --> 01:04:35.470
But you can see in these
last couple of months,

01:04:35.470 --> 01:04:39.240
every single time we
make the simplest error,

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:42.570
or a complicated
error in anatomy,

01:04:42.570 --> 01:04:44.160
he interrupts and corrects us.

01:04:44.160 --> 01:04:46.950
And he is always right.

01:04:46.950 --> 01:04:50.860
Now tell us what
actually-- where did he go?

01:04:50.860 --> 01:04:55.050
And he never had a
day's training in this.

01:04:55.050 --> 01:04:59.790
But he knew every
bit of anatomy like--

01:04:59.790 --> 01:05:03.960
the medical journals,
never mind like a person.

01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:08.700
And he had the ability to--

01:05:08.700 --> 01:05:13.590
anything that passed before
his eyes or his ears went in.

01:05:13.590 --> 01:05:16.740
Karl Wildes-- professor Wildes
in electrical engineering--

01:05:16.740 --> 01:05:21.180
in 1936 took him to a Gilbert
and Sullivan production.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:25.710
And everybody knew Wiener's
reputation from the time

01:05:25.710 --> 01:05:27.020
he was 25 on.

01:05:27.020 --> 01:05:30.030
And so Wildes came out
and said, how much of that

01:05:30.030 --> 01:05:31.290
do you remember?

01:05:31.290 --> 01:05:34.030
Wiener looked down,
what do you want?

01:05:34.030 --> 01:05:37.662
And he named a piece.

01:05:37.662 --> 01:05:39.120
And you know,
Gilbert and Sullivan,

01:05:39.120 --> 01:05:41.730
it goes so rapidly you
can barely understand it.

01:05:41.730 --> 01:05:44.610
And right through,
word for word.

01:05:44.610 --> 01:05:45.310
Just once in.

01:05:45.310 --> 01:05:46.840
That's all you needed.

01:05:46.840 --> 01:05:51.060
Well Wiener really--

01:05:51.060 --> 01:05:52.920
I don't know if he
made it a point.

01:05:52.920 --> 01:05:54.270
He must have.

01:05:54.270 --> 01:05:56.310
He worked on me
for 10 years to get

01:05:56.310 --> 01:06:01.680
me to understand and
believe that everything he

01:06:01.680 --> 01:06:04.330
was was a result of his
father's upbringing.

01:06:04.330 --> 01:06:07.240
Which he resented all
his life, by the way.

01:06:07.240 --> 01:06:09.690
But he said, without that, I--

01:06:09.690 --> 01:06:14.130
all the things that people
think that I am a genius for

01:06:14.130 --> 01:06:15.600
would never have developed.

01:06:15.600 --> 01:06:19.650
His father was a professor
of languages at Harvard.

01:06:19.650 --> 01:06:22.290
And two professors at
the turn of the century

01:06:22.290 --> 01:06:27.930
decided to bring up geniuses.

01:06:27.930 --> 01:06:31.620
If I shortcut the story,
that's what they decided to do.

01:06:31.620 --> 01:06:35.760
And Wiener, at the
age of five or six,

01:06:35.760 --> 01:06:38.760
was already reciting
the Iliad and Odyssey

01:06:38.760 --> 01:06:42.930
from memory in Greek.

01:06:42.930 --> 01:06:46.080
He knew nine languages.

01:06:46.080 --> 01:06:51.180
He-- by the way, he learned to
read, write, and speak Chinese

01:06:51.180 --> 01:06:54.340
in the six months that
he spent in China.

01:06:54.340 --> 01:06:58.070
Somewhere in the
'36, '37 time frame.

01:06:58.070 --> 01:07:00.390
And Professor Wildes went
over there the next year

01:07:00.390 --> 01:07:02.280
and had the same teacher.

01:07:02.280 --> 01:07:03.390
And Wildes told me.

01:07:03.390 --> 01:07:05.940
He said, every time
he'd go for a lesson,

01:07:05.940 --> 01:07:08.820
the teacher would say,
now Norbert Wiener,

01:07:08.820 --> 01:07:10.862
he was a good student.

01:07:10.862 --> 01:07:13.754
[LAUGHTER]

01:07:15.690 --> 01:07:21.504
So all of this ability
came when the father--

01:07:21.504 --> 01:07:22.920
the way the father
brought him up.

01:07:22.920 --> 01:07:26.541
Which was, as Wiener felt,
a terrible, terrible thing.

01:07:26.541 --> 01:07:28.290
He used to have to
stand beside his father

01:07:28.290 --> 01:07:32.730
every night while the father
was correcting work for Harvard.

01:07:32.730 --> 01:07:36.060
And the father just
ignored him, until he

01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:37.980
made the simplest
error in anything

01:07:37.980 --> 01:07:40.710
and verbally
reduced him to dust.

01:07:40.710 --> 01:07:43.800
It was a terrible,
terrible experience.

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:46.740
And then, at six years old,
he was told by the doctor

01:07:46.740 --> 01:07:48.450
that his eyes were finished.

01:07:48.450 --> 01:07:52.230
And that they would be totally
finished if he did not stay

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:53.940
away from reading for a year.

01:07:53.940 --> 01:07:55.920
Now the two professors
were competing,

01:07:55.920 --> 01:07:57.720
and they were doing
pretty well according

01:07:57.720 --> 01:07:59.070
to their measure by this time.

01:07:59.070 --> 01:08:01.440
So they decided-- the
father decided, look,

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:03.570
that's no excuse.

01:08:03.570 --> 01:08:06.690
That's a problem
you have, but we're

01:08:06.690 --> 01:08:08.550
continuing with the experiment.

01:08:08.550 --> 01:08:11.070
And he forced him to do
everything in his head.

01:08:11.070 --> 01:08:15.660
And Wiener told me that,
when he was seven years old,

01:08:15.660 --> 01:08:21.899
he, as a seven-year-old,
recognized that the whole world

01:08:21.899 --> 01:08:23.790
had just changed in his vision.

01:08:23.790 --> 01:08:26.100
That he could do
things that he couldn't

01:08:26.100 --> 01:08:29.260
dream of at the age of six.

01:08:29.260 --> 01:08:32.782
And he could recognize
that at seven years old.

01:08:32.782 --> 01:08:34.740
He could do this math--
he had to do everything

01:08:34.740 --> 01:08:38.500
in his head, his
math in his head.

01:08:38.500 --> 01:08:41.430
And he went on and he got his
bachelor's degree from Tufts

01:08:41.430 --> 01:08:45.840
at 14, the first of
two doctorates at 18.

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:50.109
And the other fellow did
exactly parallel to Wiener.

01:08:50.109 --> 01:08:54.310
It was a very
interesting thing, fact.

01:08:54.310 --> 01:08:58.220
The only thing was that
the other one, at 22,

01:08:58.220 --> 01:09:01.229
said, to hell with it all,
and became an accountant.

01:09:01.229 --> 01:09:05.700
He just decided that he wanted
a different track in life.

01:09:05.700 --> 01:09:06.990
But Wiener kept on.

01:09:06.990 --> 01:09:09.510
But he had an
insecurity as a result

01:09:09.510 --> 01:09:10.770
of that that was enormous.

01:09:10.770 --> 01:09:12.510
I mean, he'd go to
these conferences

01:09:12.510 --> 01:09:15.029
and he'd come to me afterwards.

01:09:15.029 --> 01:09:16.090
He'd say, how did I do?

01:09:16.090 --> 01:09:16.710
How did I do?

01:09:16.710 --> 01:09:19.609
My god, you know, I understood
only a certain percentage

01:09:19.609 --> 01:09:21.189
of what he was talking about.

01:09:21.189 --> 01:09:23.020
But he had to be told he was OK.

01:09:23.020 --> 01:09:27.510
He never really-- he never
really had the confidence,

01:09:27.510 --> 01:09:31.090
but he had the power
that was so enormous.

01:09:31.090 --> 01:09:33.300
And what he convinced me--

01:09:33.300 --> 01:09:37.950
and I have to believe he made
a real effort over those years.

01:09:37.950 --> 01:09:41.939
Because he said it in so
many ways so many times,

01:09:41.939 --> 01:09:44.069
that everybody has this ability.

01:09:44.069 --> 01:09:45.540
We all come with it.

01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:48.720
We just don't believe it,
and we don't develop it.

01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:50.640
Nobody develops it for us.

01:09:50.640 --> 01:09:51.899
We're already getting near?

01:09:51.899 --> 01:09:52.950
How much more?

01:09:53.766 --> 01:09:54.766
AUDIENCE: Your decision.

01:09:54.766 --> 01:09:57.490
But a quarter after.

01:09:57.490 --> 01:09:58.840
PROFESSOR: Oh boy, oh god.

01:09:58.840 --> 01:10:00.170
I spent too much time on this.

01:10:00.170 --> 01:10:02.220
OK.

01:10:02.220 --> 01:10:05.970
So what I-- that
experience was one.

01:10:05.970 --> 01:10:08.400
Then I won't go into the
other ones in any detail

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:10.810
now, because I didn't realize
how much time has been--

01:10:10.810 --> 01:10:16.590
the other one was, as a result
of that experience with Wiener,

01:10:16.590 --> 01:10:20.580
when I was asked to teach
our major course right after

01:10:20.580 --> 01:10:23.490
that in electrical engineering,
the first introductory course

01:10:23.490 --> 01:10:26.460
in network theory, my
idea was to see what you

01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:29.790
could do if you got not
at the cradle but at 19

01:10:29.790 --> 01:10:31.080
or 18 years old.

01:10:31.080 --> 01:10:32.470
What could you do?

01:10:32.470 --> 01:10:35.370
And I spent seven years
to experiment with that.

01:10:35.370 --> 01:10:37.320
And then it grew to
be a subject that

01:10:37.320 --> 01:10:39.801
was taken by-- a lot
of different courses.

01:10:39.801 --> 01:10:41.300
It grew to a third
of the institute,

01:10:41.300 --> 01:10:43.620
when EE was only at 25%.

01:10:43.620 --> 01:10:45.990
And the students worked--
oh, did they work.

01:10:45.990 --> 01:10:48.540
But they worked voluntarily.

01:10:48.540 --> 01:10:51.240
I used to have a meeting
with all the section

01:10:51.240 --> 01:10:51.990
representatives.

01:10:51.990 --> 01:10:57.500
In fact, your father was one
of the section representatives.

01:10:57.500 --> 01:11:02.580
And I would tell
the students, now

01:11:02.580 --> 01:11:04.830
look, you tell your
section representative when

01:11:04.830 --> 01:11:05.730
you've got enough.

01:11:05.730 --> 01:11:07.080
This is an exciting subject.

01:11:07.080 --> 01:11:09.330
I want to give
you more homework.

01:11:09.330 --> 01:11:11.400
And I'm going to
raise it until you

01:11:11.400 --> 01:11:14.190
tell the section representative
that's enough, and I'll stop.

01:11:14.190 --> 01:11:17.690
Well, he got up to 15
hours a week homework

01:11:17.690 --> 01:11:20.550
in a 12 hour subject.

01:11:20.550 --> 01:11:23.190
And I hope this isn't
being video recorded.

01:11:23.190 --> 01:11:28.800
And I lost my nerve
at that point.

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:30.450
How can you possibly
give 15 hours

01:11:30.450 --> 01:11:32.430
homework in a 12 hours subject?

01:11:32.430 --> 01:11:35.370
So I stopped at
that level, at 15.

01:11:35.370 --> 01:11:36.630
Well, they worked.

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:39.599
And I can tell you the end
result. That the faculty--

01:11:39.599 --> 01:11:41.640
this was a different way
of teaching the subject.

01:11:41.640 --> 01:11:43.190
But the faculty.

01:11:43.190 --> 01:11:47.040
This is where tutorials
all started off, too.

01:11:47.040 --> 01:11:48.870
I used to give a
quiz to the faculty.

01:11:48.870 --> 01:11:49.650
And I had 30--

01:11:49.650 --> 01:11:53.820
I had my total pick of
the faculty at that time.

01:11:53.820 --> 01:11:57.840
And I picked the most senior
ones, whose reputation

01:11:57.840 --> 01:11:59.300
wasn't on the line.

01:11:59.300 --> 01:12:01.530
And the rules for
the tutorial were,

01:12:01.530 --> 01:12:03.510
no faculty preparation,
students can

01:12:03.510 --> 01:12:05.670
ask whatever they want in
the general subject area,

01:12:05.670 --> 01:12:07.830
and I hope the
faculty gets stuck.

01:12:07.830 --> 01:12:10.690
And I would announce
that to both of them.

01:12:10.690 --> 01:12:12.120
Because I wanted them to see--

01:12:12.120 --> 01:12:14.820
Wiener had put
into me that, look,

01:12:14.820 --> 01:12:18.000
if you ask anybody
of his generation

01:12:18.000 --> 01:12:20.040
in mathematics where
you graduated from,

01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:22.680
you will only show
your ignorance.

01:12:22.680 --> 01:12:24.970
That's not important.

01:12:24.970 --> 01:12:28.890
You ask them who did
they graduate under.

01:12:28.890 --> 01:12:30.825
And I really thought about that.

01:12:30.825 --> 01:12:32.700
You know, he graduated--
his second doctorate

01:12:32.700 --> 01:12:34.107
was under Hardy in Cambridge.

01:12:34.107 --> 01:12:35.940
And boy, you look at
the mathematicians that

01:12:35.940 --> 01:12:37.314
graduated under
Hardy and there's

01:12:37.314 --> 01:12:40.650
something-- there's
something about their pattern

01:12:40.650 --> 01:12:44.700
of operation and thinking and
everything that is common.

01:12:44.700 --> 01:12:46.740
And what it is is
they got to work

01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:50.546
with a very
experienced person who

01:12:50.546 --> 01:12:52.920
didn't know the answer to the
problem on their doctorate.

01:12:52.920 --> 01:12:54.336
And they go down
the blind alleys,

01:12:54.336 --> 01:12:56.760
back up, come over
to another approach.

01:12:56.760 --> 01:12:59.620
Why didn't they go this way
instead of 1,000 other ways?

01:12:59.620 --> 01:13:01.920
Something of that
process, which none of us

01:13:01.920 --> 01:13:07.200
know how, at this day and
age, to teach, rubs off.

01:13:07.200 --> 01:13:12.600
And so I wanted to have
the faculty and students

01:13:12.600 --> 01:13:15.750
have a chance, in groups of 4
to 1 with the senior faculty,

01:13:15.750 --> 01:13:18.450
to experience
that, what happens.

01:13:18.450 --> 01:13:20.060
And just to tell
you one result--

01:13:20.060 --> 01:13:23.540
it happened actually
twice, but--

01:13:23.540 --> 01:13:26.150
it was Professor
Wildes again who

01:13:26.150 --> 01:13:29.750
was one of the very senior
teachers at the time.

01:13:29.750 --> 01:13:34.682
He was leading one
of the tutorials.

01:13:34.682 --> 01:13:37.140
And he walked in, and somebody
asked a question that really

01:13:37.140 --> 01:13:38.220
stumped him right off the bat.

01:13:38.220 --> 01:13:40.136
He turned the board, and
he was working on it.

01:13:40.136 --> 01:13:43.200
About 10 minutes had
gone by, he got nowhere.

01:13:43.200 --> 01:13:45.600
And one boy, who
had not spoken--

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:47.061
this was four weeks
into the term--

01:13:47.061 --> 01:13:49.560
sort of timidity raised his
hand and said, sir, couldn't you

01:13:49.560 --> 01:13:51.410
do the following thing.

01:13:51.410 --> 01:13:53.610
Wildes turned
around to the board,

01:13:53.610 --> 01:13:56.480
did it, zipped
right to the answer.

01:13:56.480 --> 01:13:58.110
And then he was--

01:13:58.110 --> 01:14:00.440
his reputation
wasn't on the line.

01:14:00.440 --> 01:14:01.710
He was a senior person.

01:14:01.710 --> 01:14:03.580
And he turned around
and told the truth.

01:14:03.580 --> 01:14:06.060
He said, no-- he was
up there 10 minutes.

01:14:06.060 --> 01:14:09.000
And he said, I was trying to
solve the problem but he said,

01:14:09.000 --> 01:14:10.500
what was in the
back of my head was,

01:14:10.500 --> 01:14:13.200
God, suppose I'm up here
at the end of the hour.

01:14:13.200 --> 01:14:15.635
Oh my god, I'm going
to look like a fool.

01:14:15.635 --> 01:14:17.760
And he said, I was trying
to get those thoughts out

01:14:17.760 --> 01:14:20.070
and trying to devote
myself to the problem.

01:14:20.070 --> 01:14:23.220
But he said, one thing I'm
certain of, if I had come up

01:14:23.220 --> 01:14:26.430
with a solution, I
would not have realized

01:14:26.430 --> 01:14:27.580
such a direct approach.

01:14:27.580 --> 01:14:29.990
He said that just wasn't
in my thinking pattern.

01:14:29.990 --> 01:14:32.820
He said, this about the
most direct way you can go.

01:14:32.820 --> 01:14:35.070
Well, it turns out,
he didn't know,

01:14:35.070 --> 01:14:38.460
nor did I know, that that boy
who timidly raised his hand,

01:14:38.460 --> 01:14:40.620
he hadn't spoken in
the first four weeks.

01:14:40.620 --> 01:14:43.450
He was the one out of the
four that hadn't spoken.

01:14:43.450 --> 01:14:45.660
He was on academic
probation, to be thrown out

01:14:45.660 --> 01:14:49.140
at the end of the term.

01:14:49.140 --> 01:14:52.830
Well, it turns out that in
six weeks was the first quiz.

01:14:52.830 --> 01:14:57.540
Then there was the second one
and the third one and a final.

01:14:57.540 --> 01:15:00.210
He got 100 in the second quiz--

01:15:00.210 --> 01:15:03.150
first quiz, second quiz,
third quiz and the final exam.

01:15:03.150 --> 01:15:07.500
He was top of 300
students at MIT.

01:15:07.500 --> 01:15:10.680
He soared off of this probation.

01:15:10.680 --> 01:15:15.540
All his subjects picked up,
and he just sailed through MIT.

01:15:15.540 --> 01:15:19.860
Now, nobody educated that
person in those few moments.

01:15:19.860 --> 01:15:24.210
They only unlocked the key
that made the fellow believe

01:15:24.210 --> 01:15:27.830
that, gee, I'm no dummy.

01:15:27.830 --> 01:15:31.010
I not only solved the problem
the professor admitted

01:15:31.010 --> 01:15:35.714
he couldn't solve,
but he told me

01:15:35.714 --> 01:15:37.380
that I solved it in
a more direct method

01:15:37.380 --> 01:15:39.760
than he would have
even solved it.

01:15:39.760 --> 01:15:43.020
He believed, all of
a sudden, in himself.

01:15:43.020 --> 01:15:46.680
And that was the key
that took off for him.

01:15:46.680 --> 01:15:50.970
And I've seen that happen so
many times in different ways

01:15:50.970 --> 01:15:54.270
with people in MIT,
outside of MIT.

01:15:54.270 --> 01:15:58.840
Some key turns the person
around and they go after that.

01:15:58.840 --> 01:16:04.740
So what I really dearly
want you to understand

01:16:04.740 --> 01:16:07.230
is that you all have that.

01:16:07.230 --> 01:16:08.910
And if you can believe it--

01:16:08.910 --> 01:16:10.892
I mean, I wish I
had more time to--

01:16:10.892 --> 01:16:13.350
the last, by the way, was the
[INAUDIBLE],, but most of you

01:16:13.350 --> 01:16:15.730
have heard me give
the introduction.

01:16:15.730 --> 01:16:18.240
Which he takes people
who were selling drugs,

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:20.070
who couldn't give a
dime, and their parents

01:16:20.070 --> 01:16:22.920
give a dime for arithmetic.

01:16:22.920 --> 01:16:26.970
And turns them into scholars
that go on through calculus,

01:16:26.970 --> 01:16:30.750
chemistry, physics, everything.

01:16:30.750 --> 01:16:34.650
And just if I could get you
to believe that that's there.

01:16:34.650 --> 01:16:36.900
And when things get
rough-- and they will.

01:16:36.900 --> 01:16:39.090
And life is somewhat of a yo yo.

01:16:39.090 --> 01:16:43.180
It presents problems that
really depress you at times,

01:16:43.180 --> 01:16:46.740
and you get pretty high
on things at other times.

01:16:46.740 --> 01:16:50.370
By the way, since
I'm having to cut

01:16:50.370 --> 01:16:56.070
a lot of this short now, one
thing I would ask you is that,

01:16:56.070 --> 01:17:00.480
concentrate on getting
your satisfaction in life

01:17:00.480 --> 01:17:03.150
from what you think
about what you've done,

01:17:03.150 --> 01:17:05.100
not from what others think.

01:17:05.100 --> 01:17:06.690
What others think is fickle.

01:17:06.690 --> 01:17:09.210
It goes up and
down all the time.

01:17:09.210 --> 01:17:10.530
You see this.

01:17:10.530 --> 01:17:13.020
Actors have great
depression when

01:17:13.020 --> 01:17:14.910
they fall out of popularity.

01:17:14.910 --> 01:17:17.940
Sports people go
through the same thing.

01:17:17.940 --> 01:17:24.460
Because they're looking for
their satisfaction from others.

01:17:24.460 --> 01:17:29.920
Those of you that learn
to get it from yourself--

01:17:29.920 --> 01:17:32.130
you know everybody has
some measure inside of them

01:17:32.130 --> 01:17:33.760
when they're doing well
and when they're not.

01:17:33.760 --> 01:17:35.510
And if you get that
satisfaction from when

01:17:35.510 --> 01:17:41.080
you are doing something
well, that is lasting.

01:17:41.080 --> 01:17:45.400
And it is the only form
that will really help you.

01:17:45.400 --> 01:17:47.660
And it's really exciting.

01:17:47.660 --> 01:17:52.366
There's nothing like having done
something and worked and worked

01:17:52.366 --> 01:17:53.740
and worked on it--
let's say it's

01:17:53.740 --> 01:17:59.180
research, which excites me--
and you get a result, finally.

01:17:59.180 --> 01:18:02.290
And it's like going through
many, many, many blind alleys

01:18:02.290 --> 01:18:04.210
and you finally get something.

01:18:04.210 --> 01:18:06.400
That satisfaction is enormous.

01:18:06.400 --> 01:18:10.040
And there's another degree of it
which shouldn't be what it is.

01:18:10.040 --> 01:18:12.420
If what you were trying
to do was impossible--

01:18:12.420 --> 01:18:14.650
was thought to be
impossible by others--

01:18:14.650 --> 01:18:16.410
you get more satisfaction.

01:18:16.410 --> 01:18:18.350
That's pure ego.

01:18:18.350 --> 01:18:19.700
It shouldn't be, but it is.

01:18:19.700 --> 01:18:20.560
It's there.

01:18:20.560 --> 01:18:22.390
So if you're doing
something that you're

01:18:22.390 --> 01:18:26.360
working on that is thought
to be impossible-- in fact,

01:18:26.360 --> 01:18:28.670
[INAUDIBLE] in his
classroom has this big sign

01:18:28.670 --> 01:18:29.420
that he'd painted.

01:18:29.420 --> 01:18:32.560
And it says, if you think
something is impossible,

01:18:32.560 --> 01:18:34.750
do not disturb the
person who is doing it.

01:18:37.280 --> 01:18:38.620
Good thinking.

01:18:38.620 --> 01:18:43.160
Now I'm really going
to cut short here.

01:18:43.160 --> 01:18:45.670
The other thing I would like
to just tell you very quickly

01:18:45.670 --> 01:18:47.110
about looking at a job.

01:18:47.110 --> 01:18:49.180
You heard a lot of it
from our people today.

01:18:49.180 --> 01:18:52.810
Look for the people that you're
going to be working with.

01:18:52.810 --> 01:18:53.680
Very important.

01:18:53.680 --> 01:18:56.140
Because what you will
be will be a reflection

01:18:56.140 --> 01:19:00.310
directly of the people that
you come in contact with.

01:19:00.310 --> 01:19:02.740
If they are high level,
you will do better.

01:19:02.740 --> 01:19:04.845
Same when you pick a school.

01:19:04.845 --> 01:19:07.672
When you pick a school,
really the way to do it

01:19:07.672 --> 01:19:09.130
is pick the school
with the highest

01:19:09.130 --> 01:19:11.680
level of students in it.

01:19:11.680 --> 01:19:16.810
Because that causes you to
rise to meet that level.

01:19:16.810 --> 01:19:18.414
And that is extremely important.

01:19:18.414 --> 01:19:20.080
The other thing is
look at the character

01:19:20.080 --> 01:19:21.640
as well as the
ability of the people.

01:19:21.640 --> 01:19:28.740
When you go interviewing,
you ask a lot of questions.

01:19:28.740 --> 01:19:31.770
This whole business
of going for a job

01:19:31.770 --> 01:19:35.302
is like having the
permission to have one date.

01:19:35.302 --> 01:19:36.760
And then after the
date, you've got

01:19:36.760 --> 01:19:40.210
to decide if you're
going to get married.

01:19:40.210 --> 01:19:41.770
And what happens
on the one date?

01:19:41.770 --> 01:19:45.559
You're both on
your best behavior.

01:19:45.559 --> 01:19:47.350
And so the company that
you're interviewing

01:19:47.350 --> 01:19:48.975
is on their best
behavior, because they

01:19:48.975 --> 01:19:50.356
are looking for somebody.

01:19:50.356 --> 01:19:51.730
And you're on your
best behavior,

01:19:51.730 --> 01:19:53.438
because you want to
show them that you're

01:19:53.438 --> 01:19:55.660
the person they're looking for.

01:19:55.660 --> 01:19:58.270
And you learn very little.

01:19:58.270 --> 01:20:00.932
Do a few things.

01:20:00.932 --> 01:20:03.390
That won't be expected by the
companies you're interviewing

01:20:03.390 --> 01:20:06.089
but, for example, you
spend a lot of time

01:20:06.089 --> 01:20:07.630
with the person who
takes you around.

01:20:07.630 --> 01:20:09.840
Ask them what their
history is in the company.

01:20:09.840 --> 01:20:14.280
When they joined it and
what happened to them,

01:20:14.280 --> 01:20:17.310
how they view it.

01:20:17.310 --> 01:20:21.300
Then get down to asking,
what are the three things you

01:20:21.300 --> 01:20:23.200
like the most?

01:20:23.200 --> 01:20:25.620
And what are the three
things you like the least?

01:20:25.620 --> 01:20:29.760
Pay attention to the first one,
on what you like the least.

01:20:29.760 --> 01:20:32.200
That's usually the thing
that's central in their head.

01:20:32.200 --> 01:20:35.190
And when you get interviewed
by people, ask them that.

01:20:35.190 --> 01:20:36.060
That's all right.

01:20:36.060 --> 01:20:38.250
No problem with
asking questions.

01:20:38.250 --> 01:20:40.390
And they're not
prepared for that.

01:20:40.390 --> 01:20:43.770
But you will see that,
like in any company,

01:20:43.770 --> 01:20:45.899
there are advantages
and disadvantages.

01:20:45.899 --> 01:20:47.190
And you want to have a balance.

01:20:47.190 --> 01:20:48.660
You want to understand that.

01:20:48.660 --> 01:20:50.827
So don't worry about asking
those kind of questions.

01:20:50.827 --> 01:20:53.034
What do you like-- what are
the three things you like

01:20:53.034 --> 01:20:54.370
the most about the company?

01:20:54.370 --> 01:20:56.828
What are three things you like
the least about the company?

01:20:56.828 --> 01:20:59.220
You get a very good idea.

01:20:59.220 --> 01:21:02.310
And look for-- pardon me.

01:21:02.310 --> 01:21:04.410
Look for the
character as well as

01:21:04.410 --> 01:21:06.207
the technical
ability of the people

01:21:06.207 --> 01:21:07.290
you're going to work with.

01:21:07.290 --> 01:21:09.260
Very, very important.

01:21:09.260 --> 01:21:12.420
Unfortunately, there's a heck
of a lot of, in this world,

01:21:12.420 --> 01:21:15.860
very poor ethics that extend
through corporations also.

01:21:15.860 --> 01:21:20.640
Many corporations-- I wonder
if it's not most corporations.

01:21:20.640 --> 01:21:22.710
If you are caught
up in something

01:21:22.710 --> 01:21:25.860
like that, your
ethics, your principles

01:21:25.860 --> 01:21:28.800
will change in the
wrong direction.

01:21:28.800 --> 01:21:31.090
Directions that you wouldn't
like them to change.

01:21:31.090 --> 01:21:32.530
But it will happen.

01:21:32.530 --> 01:21:35.850
So watch those elements
as much as you can.

01:21:35.850 --> 01:21:39.090
And then the last thing
I would say very quickly

01:21:39.090 --> 01:21:43.260
about companies is,
you will encounter,

01:21:43.260 --> 01:21:45.630
in every organization,
not just companies--

01:21:45.630 --> 01:21:49.350
whether you join a church or
whether you join an industry,

01:21:49.350 --> 01:21:52.900
you will encounter
politics of people.

01:21:52.900 --> 01:21:57.209
And that perhaps-- well,
I can't say a priori.

01:21:57.209 --> 01:21:59.500
I mean, you're either going
to be part of the solution.

01:21:59.500 --> 01:22:02.131
You're going to be indifferent
to it, which is pretty hard,

01:22:02.131 --> 01:22:03.880
or you're going to be
part of the problem.

01:22:03.880 --> 01:22:07.030
I hope it's not that.

01:22:07.030 --> 01:22:10.090
It happens usually
with weak people.

01:22:10.090 --> 01:22:14.440
Who, for protection, for
security, build an empire.

01:22:14.440 --> 01:22:17.192
And they build this with--

01:22:17.192 --> 01:22:18.400
if you want to say, intrigue.

01:22:18.400 --> 01:22:19.983
I don't know what
you want to call it,

01:22:19.983 --> 01:22:23.770
but they build a
protection around them.

01:22:23.770 --> 01:22:27.940
They usually bring weak
people in around them also.

01:22:27.940 --> 01:22:30.700
Because they will be shown
up by a strong person.

01:22:30.700 --> 01:22:35.950
These kinds of things are the
essence of what can destroy

01:22:35.950 --> 01:22:40.100
not only the work
but the people.

01:22:40.100 --> 01:22:41.004
Whether we think of--

01:22:41.004 --> 01:22:42.670
you probably haven't
thought about this,

01:22:42.670 --> 01:22:47.590
but most of the waking hours
that you will have in life

01:22:47.590 --> 01:22:50.680
will be in some way
associated with work.

01:22:50.680 --> 01:22:53.560
Being there, going to it, coming
from it, thinking about it.

01:22:53.560 --> 01:22:55.170
You can't help it.

01:22:55.170 --> 01:22:57.220
And it's true for
almost everybody.

01:22:57.220 --> 01:22:58.970
In the professional
field, it's very true.

01:22:58.970 --> 01:23:02.660
You can't turn the switch off at
five o'clock or whatever it is.

01:23:02.660 --> 01:23:04.780
And if that's a
pleasant experience,

01:23:04.780 --> 01:23:06.850
your life will be very pleasant.

01:23:06.850 --> 01:23:11.050
If it's a bad experience, you'll
be going through life in a way

01:23:11.050 --> 01:23:12.910
that you really shouldn't.

01:23:12.910 --> 01:23:15.310
Maybe you've heard the
story of the beggar who

01:23:15.310 --> 01:23:18.780
sat on the corner, and there
was a well-dressed businessmen

01:23:18.780 --> 01:23:20.980
who he noticed was
rushing by every morning

01:23:20.980 --> 01:23:21.820
at a certain time.

01:23:21.820 --> 01:23:25.550
And one morning, the businessman
was going a little slower.

01:23:25.550 --> 01:23:28.172
And so the beggar got
up and thought maybe

01:23:28.172 --> 01:23:29.130
he could interrupt him.

01:23:29.130 --> 01:23:32.530
And he said, sir, he says, I
see you going here every day.

01:23:32.530 --> 01:23:34.060
What are you doing?

01:23:34.060 --> 01:23:36.650
And the fellow says,
I'm going to work.

01:23:36.650 --> 01:23:39.130
And the beggar says, why?

01:23:39.130 --> 01:23:41.100
And he says, so
I can earn money.

01:23:41.100 --> 01:23:43.970
The beggar says, why?

01:23:43.970 --> 01:23:49.400
Well, so I can save for
this and I can retire.

01:23:49.400 --> 01:23:51.475
And he said, well, why
do you want to retire?

01:23:51.475 --> 01:23:53.570
He said, so I can
do what I want.

01:23:53.570 --> 01:23:58.840
The beggar said, Jesus,
that's what I'm doing now.

01:23:58.840 --> 01:24:02.154
So don't go through
life trying to focus

01:24:02.154 --> 01:24:04.570
on what you're going to get
that will make it comfortable.

01:24:04.570 --> 01:24:08.020
Go through life
making everything

01:24:08.020 --> 01:24:15.280
you do interesting to
you, and you will perform.

01:24:15.280 --> 01:24:17.105
You will perform
very, very well.

01:24:17.105 --> 01:24:19.480
I've always found that the
people who come into a company

01:24:19.480 --> 01:24:23.970
and want a position or
power or money, their focus

01:24:23.970 --> 01:24:26.470
is so much on that the
creativity part usually

01:24:26.470 --> 01:24:27.656
doesn't come along.

01:24:27.656 --> 01:24:29.530
The people who are
totally excited about what

01:24:29.530 --> 01:24:33.410
they're doing, creativity
just bubbles out.

01:24:33.410 --> 01:24:35.500
And if they're in the
right corporation, my god,

01:24:35.500 --> 01:24:36.400
they get recognized.

01:24:36.400 --> 01:24:37.930
They never have to
blow their horn.

01:24:37.930 --> 01:24:42.160
The good ones never, never,
ever have to blow their horn.

01:24:42.160 --> 01:24:44.140
Now, one last thing.

01:24:44.140 --> 01:24:47.212
Bethany, if you're not
here, you won't [INAUDIBLE]..

01:24:49.930 --> 01:24:51.680
A little bit about creativity.

01:24:51.680 --> 01:24:54.100
The best way to get this going.

01:24:54.100 --> 01:24:57.150
Einstein had this theory that--

01:24:57.150 --> 01:25:00.260
and I really believe that,
that the mind has many levels.

01:25:00.260 --> 01:25:01.740
It's capable of
working on problems

01:25:01.740 --> 01:25:03.281
at all these different
levels, and it

01:25:03.281 --> 01:25:06.040
fires to the equivalent
of an output register

01:25:06.040 --> 01:25:07.180
when you have an idea.

01:25:07.180 --> 01:25:09.160
And that's when you get
a flash and you think,

01:25:09.160 --> 01:25:12.094
oh boy, then you spend a
long, long time trying to--

01:25:12.094 --> 01:25:13.510
all the tools that
you've learned,

01:25:13.510 --> 01:25:16.310
justify that that
thing really works.

01:25:16.310 --> 01:25:19.649
And the real question comes,
and the exciting thing

01:25:19.649 --> 01:25:21.190
comes, how do you
get that mind going

01:25:21.190 --> 01:25:22.273
on those different levels?

01:25:25.240 --> 01:25:28.600
And I believe there
is a very definite way

01:25:28.600 --> 01:25:32.190
to do that, a very definite way.

01:25:32.190 --> 01:25:35.640
And that is,
whatever your job is,

01:25:35.640 --> 01:25:41.460
you decide that
you're going to do it

01:25:41.460 --> 01:25:44.445
better than it's ever been done
by anyone else or yourself.

01:25:45.780 --> 01:25:48.240
And the minute you have
decided that that's

01:25:48.240 --> 01:25:50.970
what you're going to
do, what happens is you

01:25:50.970 --> 01:25:54.361
immediately-- like a sponge,
you learn what's been done.

01:25:54.361 --> 01:25:56.610
And then you think about it
and you say, well, how can

01:25:56.610 --> 01:25:58.680
I do it better?

01:25:58.680 --> 01:26:02.850
And that starts the creativity
process as quickly as

01:26:02.850 --> 01:26:05.010
when somebody drops
that puzzle in your lap

01:26:05.010 --> 01:26:06.190
and you don't care about it.

01:26:06.190 --> 01:26:07.856
And then you start
working, and then you

01:26:07.856 --> 01:26:09.760
can't see anything else.

01:26:09.760 --> 01:26:15.397
And when you see things that
don't work, for example.

01:26:15.397 --> 01:26:17.730
You get into a car and the
windshield wiper is smearing.

01:26:17.730 --> 01:26:18.780
OK.

01:26:18.780 --> 01:26:22.385
Don't think, Jesus, what a
miserable design that is.

01:26:22.385 --> 01:26:23.760
I want you to instead--

01:26:23.760 --> 01:26:26.910
I wonder how it
could be done better.

01:26:26.910 --> 01:26:28.470
If you try to do
something better--

01:26:28.470 --> 01:26:31.770
it's impossible to do
anything better that you

01:26:31.770 --> 01:26:33.210
haven't done differently, OK?

01:26:33.210 --> 01:26:35.860
If it's not different, it's
impossible to be better.

01:26:35.860 --> 01:26:38.130
The inverse isn't
exactly true, and you

01:26:38.130 --> 01:26:39.970
see a lot of examples of that.

01:26:39.970 --> 01:26:44.980
But if you do something better,
it will always be different.

01:26:44.980 --> 01:26:48.750
And if you simply decide that.

01:26:48.750 --> 01:26:51.690
And the other thing is that
you're going to do it better.

01:26:51.690 --> 01:26:53.700
You will start the mind going.

01:26:53.700 --> 01:26:55.860
And how do you know
if it's working?

01:26:55.860 --> 01:26:59.100
How do you know if your
mind really is working?

01:26:59.100 --> 01:27:01.620
When you start getting things
in the middle of the night,

01:27:01.620 --> 01:27:04.200
you wake up, you get an idea,
or you're shaving or doing

01:27:04.200 --> 01:27:05.640
something, and you get an idea.

01:27:05.640 --> 01:27:07.650
You're driving, you get an idea.

01:27:07.650 --> 01:27:09.870
That's when-- I am
convinced that's

01:27:09.870 --> 01:27:12.180
when that process is working.

01:27:12.180 --> 01:27:15.120
If you don't get any
of that, you really

01:27:15.120 --> 01:27:17.520
haven't had the
desire to do something

01:27:17.520 --> 01:27:20.510
that's different and better.

01:27:20.510 --> 01:27:22.050
Everybody can do it.

01:27:22.050 --> 01:27:26.600
That's what you've got to
get that mechanism going.

01:27:26.600 --> 01:27:28.130
And it's not hard.

01:27:28.130 --> 01:27:31.380
It really isn't hard.

01:27:31.380 --> 01:27:34.390
I once asked
Wiener, when we were

01:27:34.390 --> 01:27:38.020
walking from the faculty club
into the parking lot one day.

01:27:38.020 --> 01:27:41.320
I said, how come you've made
such contributions in so

01:27:41.320 --> 01:27:43.180
many different fields?

01:27:43.180 --> 01:27:45.340
And he answered just two words.

01:27:45.340 --> 01:27:48.710
He said, insatiable curiosity.

01:27:51.600 --> 01:27:54.940
He always was
wondering why or how

01:27:54.940 --> 01:27:56.770
you could do something better.

01:27:56.770 --> 01:28:00.280
That's the key in all of this.

01:28:00.280 --> 01:28:02.822
So focus on this kind of thing.

01:28:02.822 --> 01:28:04.780
Don't worry about all
the things that you think

01:28:04.780 --> 01:28:07.480
are necessary in life
and they'll come.

01:28:07.480 --> 01:28:08.620
The money, everything else.

01:28:08.620 --> 01:28:11.260
I never have worried
one day about anything

01:28:11.260 --> 01:28:15.220
like that, when I didn't
have any money or when I did.

01:28:15.220 --> 01:28:18.960
I've never even decided my
own salary in this company.

01:28:18.960 --> 01:28:21.820
We have a firm outside
that makes surveys.

01:28:21.820 --> 01:28:24.932
But things go well, and you
have all of those things.

01:28:24.932 --> 01:28:26.890
None of those will bring
happiness, by the way.

01:28:26.890 --> 01:28:28.973
You've been told that since
you were a little kid,

01:28:28.973 --> 01:28:32.440
but I can guarantee that I have
a pretty wide circle of people

01:28:32.440 --> 01:28:33.880
and that doesn't bring it.

01:28:33.880 --> 01:28:35.620
What brings it is
to your satisfaction

01:28:35.620 --> 01:28:39.370
of doing something that's
really exciting and better.

01:28:39.370 --> 01:28:42.660
And you can all have that, and
you're all in a super position

01:28:42.660 --> 01:28:43.456
to get it.

01:28:47.110 --> 01:28:52.680
Well, lest I lose my job here,
I think we'd better close up.

01:28:52.680 --> 01:28:55.458
[LAUGHTER]

01:28:58.240 --> 01:29:05.640
In closing, I will
simply share with you

01:29:05.640 --> 01:29:09.470
a feeling that I've had for
quite a number of years.

01:29:09.470 --> 01:29:11.110
And that is every--

01:29:11.110 --> 01:29:13.300
quite frankly, every
September rolls around

01:29:13.300 --> 01:29:18.280
and I say, oh my god, why did
I commit to teaching this fall?

01:29:18.280 --> 01:29:21.636
How am I ever going to
fit this in my schedule?

01:29:21.636 --> 01:29:25.840
And oh, you know, it looks
like a huge load coming on.

01:29:26.920 --> 01:29:32.140
And every December, I look
at the class and I say--

01:29:32.140 --> 01:29:35.940
it's a funny kind
of thing by the way.

01:29:35.940 --> 01:29:38.950
I don't know many of you at all.

01:29:38.950 --> 01:29:40.940
But you feel that you do.

01:29:40.940 --> 01:29:42.700
You come into the
same room regularly

01:29:42.700 --> 01:29:45.680
and you see the same faces,
even if you just scan them.

01:29:45.680 --> 01:29:47.420
It becomes like a big family.

01:29:47.420 --> 01:29:50.890
And every time that
December rolls around,

01:29:50.890 --> 01:29:54.200
I sort of feel sad that
the thing is finishing.

01:29:54.940 --> 01:29:58.360
And it causes me-- and it has
caused me for many, many years

01:29:58.360 --> 01:30:05.150
to say, oh I'll do
it just once more.

01:30:05.150 --> 01:30:08.320
And that always has happened,
and it happens again now

01:30:08.320 --> 01:30:10.430
in December.

01:30:10.430 --> 01:30:15.310
So you're no different, and I
thank you for that experience.

01:30:15.310 --> 01:30:15.910
All the best.

01:30:15.910 --> 01:30:20.460
[APPLAUSE]

